1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,870

2
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:08,180
All right, so, hey, guys.

3
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:10,400
Thanks for coming
to our second class.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,850
Today we have the pleasure
of welcoming Fei Xia.

5
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:16,640
He's a senior research scientist
at Google DeepMind, where

6
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:18,680
he works on the robotics team.

7
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:21,050
He received his
PhD here, actually,

8
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:24,620
working with Silvio
Saverese and Stanford Vision

9
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:27,740
and Learning Lab, as
well as [INAUDIBLE]..

10
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:31,730
And his mission is to build
intelligent, embodied agents

11
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:34,760
that can interact with complex
and unstructured real world

12
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:39,620
environments with applications
like home robotics.

13
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:41,270
And recently, he
has been exploring

14
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:45,680
the use of foundation models for
robot decision making and action

15
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:46,850
generation.

16
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:50,090
So now I'll hand it off to Fei.

17
00:00:50,090 --> 00:00:51,080
Hi, everyone.

18
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,540
I'm super happy to be
here and happy to be back.

19
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:55,820
I graduated from
here two years ago.

20
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:58,940
And now I'm a research
scientist at Google DeepMind.

21
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:00,410
I work on the robotics team.

22
00:01:00,410 --> 00:01:03,290
And today I will be talking
about "Low-level Embodied

23
00:01:03,290 --> 00:01:05,500
Intelligence with
Foundation Models."

24
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,530
So it's definitely
an interesting topic.

25
00:01:08,530 --> 00:01:11,460
And I will introduce what
is embodied intelligence

26
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:13,530
and what is low-level
embodied intelligence,

27
00:01:13,530 --> 00:01:17,430
and how we can accelerate
the building of them

28
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:20,760
with foundation models.

29
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:24,340
All right, so why are we working
on embodied intelligence?

30
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:27,810
So embodied intelligence
is an integral part

31
00:01:27,810 --> 00:01:30,480
of artificial intelligence.

32
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:34,680
And it's an important
milestone to artificial general

33
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:35,500
intelligence.

34
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:37,440
And it has a lot of use cases.

35
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:41,145
Like, for example, we all hope
we have a home robot that can be

36
00:01:41,145 --> 00:01:44,520
in our home 24/7 and
clean the home for us,

37
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:49,050
or clean up our messy
room, or cook for us,

38
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:52,300
or take care of our
aging family members.

39
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:53,890
So we are not quite there yet.

40
00:01:53,890 --> 00:01:56,160
In fact, we are
quite far from it.

41
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:59,790
That is because our
intelligence is currently mostly

42
00:01:59,790 --> 00:02:00,880
in the virtual world.

43
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:05,640
So we have AI agents that can
help us draft emails, or write

44
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:07,980
eloquent essays,
but they are not

45
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:11,430
super good at interacting
with the messy, real world,

46
00:02:11,430 --> 00:02:13,740
unstructured,
complex environment

47
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:16,320
that a human resides in.

48
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:19,440
So just to give you guys
a couple of examples

49
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,720
of how messy the
real world can be

50
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:24,690
and how hostile it
could be to robotics,

51
00:02:24,690 --> 00:02:29,340
I want to show you a curious
mistake or curious error

52
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:30,730
from one of our robots.

53
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:34,440
So the task is to put
the Coke can in the sink.

54
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:36,340
And watch what the robot does.

55
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:39,190
The robot grabs the Coke
can and opens the tap.

56
00:02:39,190 --> 00:02:45,690
So this is kind of dangerous,
but it's kind of interesting,

57
00:02:45,690 --> 00:02:48,880
because we never expect it
would do something like that.

58
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:50,530
It's just from random noise.

59
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:54,130
It starts to open the tap, and
the water starts coming out.

60
00:02:54,130 --> 00:02:59,090
So for an agent to have this
type of physical intelligence,

61
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:02,160
it needs to understand
the effect of its actions

62
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:04,750
and what is so-called
a world model.

63
00:03:04,750 --> 00:03:07,710
So people have been complaining
that language models so far

64
00:03:07,710 --> 00:03:08,860
don't have a world model.

65
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:11,380
So it doesn't
understand geometry.

66
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:14,850
It doesn't understand the
spatial relationship of objects

67
00:03:14,850 --> 00:03:17,700
or the effect of
actions, basically how

68
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:21,510
an object will move
according to physical laws.

69
00:03:21,510 --> 00:03:24,120
So we are not quite there yet.

70
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:27,570
In another case--
so this is our robot

71
00:03:27,570 --> 00:03:31,300
that is ready to deliver a can,
or actually throw away a can.

72
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:34,770
But as you can see, we have
this pre-programmed behavior

73
00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:37,000
of tucking the arm behind.

74
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,880
And in doing that, the
can is upside down.

75
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:42,300
So if there is any
liquid in the can,

76
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:44,710
it will spill and
damage the robot.

77
00:03:44,710 --> 00:03:48,300
So it's another example of
real world is really complex,

78
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:50,320
and there are a lot
of things to model.

79
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:54,150
And in order for
our robots to have

80
00:03:54,150 --> 00:03:56,220
this sort of ambient
intelligence,

81
00:03:56,220 --> 00:04:00,510
it really needs to understand
a lot of very nuanced details

82
00:04:00,510 --> 00:04:02,130
of the environment
and understanding

83
00:04:02,130 --> 00:04:06,090
the physics, physical laws,
and understanding effect

84
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:09,120
of its actions.

85
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:10,010
How do we do that?

86
00:04:10,010 --> 00:04:12,340
There are many ways to
achieve embodied intelligence.

87
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:14,820
Actually, throughout
my PhD study,

88
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:18,269
I've been fascinated by
this idea of creating

89
00:04:18,269 --> 00:04:19,660
interactive environments.

90
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:22,288
Basically, let an
agent explore in

91
00:04:22,288 --> 00:04:23,580
these interactive environments.

92
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:28,170
Basically, create environments
that are complex enough

93
00:04:28,170 --> 00:04:31,830
so that if the agent needs to
survive in such environment,

94
00:04:31,830 --> 00:04:33,760
it must develop intelligence.

95
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:37,200
So it's an ecological view
of perception and agency,

96
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:41,910
and it's popularized by American
psychologist James J. Gibson.

97
00:04:41,910 --> 00:04:46,710
So he has a famous quote, "Ask
not what is inside your head,

98
00:04:46,710 --> 00:04:48,700
but what your head
is inside of."

99
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:51,275
So humans learn this type
of embodied intelligence.

100
00:04:51,275 --> 00:04:55,650
A human is able to manipulate
objects effortlessly,

101
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:57,270
one, because of
evolution, second,

102
00:04:57,270 --> 00:04:59,310
because the
childhood experience.

103
00:04:59,310 --> 00:05:00,937
We have been playing
with this toy.

104
00:05:00,937 --> 00:05:02,520
We have been interacting
with this toy

105
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:05,700
and watched the physical
effect so that we learn.

106
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:09,810
And similarly, we can
give robots a safe playpen

107
00:05:09,810 --> 00:05:13,050
so they can explore
in those environment,

108
00:05:13,050 --> 00:05:14,490
and interact with
the environment,

109
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:17,280
and play, and watch
the effect of actions,

110
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:22,140
and effectively understand how
to manipulate those objects.

111
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:27,300
So I have been developing
this simulation environment,

112
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:30,150
one of which is called a
Gibson environment, which

113
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:32,160
is published as CVPR.

114
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:37,680
It's mainly aiming at simulating
the visual world faithfully

115
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:40,810
and also simulate the
physical world to some extent.

116
00:05:40,810 --> 00:05:44,670
So we built this environment,
which is a scanned environment,

117
00:05:44,670 --> 00:05:47,610
from a lot of houses,
and then an agent,

118
00:05:47,610 --> 00:05:51,450
we can spawn an agent in that,
in this case, a humanoid agent,

119
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:56,520
and the agent can learn to walk,
or to run in this environment,

120
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:58,830
and simulate all this
perception information.

121
00:05:58,830 --> 00:06:03,490
So we can create a perception
action loop for this agent.

122
00:06:03,490 --> 00:06:06,240
And similarly, we can
put other types of agent

123
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:07,510
in this environment.

124
00:06:07,510 --> 00:06:10,910
In this case, a little cart.

125
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:14,680
And we can also put a
quadruped, or this ant

126
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:15,950
into this environment.

127
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:19,210
So essentially, we
create an environment

128
00:06:19,210 --> 00:06:22,840
where we can simulate
perception for the agent,

129
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,820
and then we can create
a neural network

130
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:26,510
to map the perception to action.

131
00:06:26,510 --> 00:06:30,800
And this way, we achieve some
sort of physical intelligence.

132
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:35,610
It's mostly for
navigation and locomotion.

133
00:06:35,610 --> 00:06:37,320
This is not enough.

134
00:06:37,320 --> 00:06:42,420
So in this case, the environment
is one monolithic piece of mesh.

135
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:45,060
As you can see, the
agent ran into the wall,

136
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:46,750
and it bounced back.

137
00:06:46,750 --> 00:06:49,900
So there's no articulation
in this environment.

138
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:53,650
So it's not simulating the full
complexity of the environment.

139
00:06:53,650 --> 00:06:57,270
So the things that we
can do with our agent

140
00:06:57,270 --> 00:06:58,740
is rather limited.

141
00:06:58,740 --> 00:07:01,830
So that's why we create other
simulation environments,

142
00:07:01,830 --> 00:07:04,320
one of which is iGibson
environment, which

143
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:05,820
is called Interactive Gibson.

144
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:08,970
So what we do is we create--

145
00:07:08,970 --> 00:07:12,630
we, again, scan a lot
of real world houses,

146
00:07:12,630 --> 00:07:15,660
and then we convert
them to CAD assets,

147
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:18,460
basically, mesh assets
that are interactable.

148
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:21,270
In this case, we have
a simulated agent

149
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:24,960
that goes into the environment,
and then closes all the drawers.

150
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:28,350
So we are able to do that
because we model the complexity

151
00:07:28,350 --> 00:07:30,370
of the world a little bit more.

152
00:07:30,370 --> 00:07:32,980
We go beyond just
modeling the visual world.

153
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:35,140
We start to model physics
a little bit more.

154
00:07:35,140 --> 00:07:39,710
Basically, modeling the degree
of freedom in the environment,

155
00:07:39,710 --> 00:07:43,750
and our agent can do more
than just navigating around.

156
00:07:43,750 --> 00:07:46,990
So we can go even further.

157
00:07:46,990 --> 00:07:49,780
So we can even model
more degrees of freedom.

158
00:07:49,780 --> 00:07:52,840
And our agent can develop
more complicated behavior,

159
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:55,810
such as unloading a
dishwasher and finding a bowl,

160
00:07:55,810 --> 00:07:59,660
or take out a bowl, and
put it on the table.

161
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:02,410
So as we scale up the
complexity of the environment,

162
00:08:02,410 --> 00:08:07,420
we are able to learn much
more complicated skills

163
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:08,750
in the simulation.

164
00:08:08,750 --> 00:08:13,110
And that's one way to achieve
embodied intelligence, which

165
00:08:13,110 --> 00:08:15,730
is to build a complex enough
simulation environment.

166
00:08:15,730 --> 00:08:18,880

167
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:22,480
Not just in my research, but the
entire field of computer vision

168
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:24,460
is undergoing a paradigm shift.

169
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:27,670
So previously, we are
focusing on internet AI.

170
00:08:27,670 --> 00:08:30,190
We curate a lot of
internet data sets

171
00:08:30,190 --> 00:08:34,059
to study problems, like
classification, segmentation,

172
00:08:34,059 --> 00:08:34,789
and detection.

173
00:08:34,789 --> 00:08:37,070
Basically, all these
computer vision problems.

174
00:08:37,070 --> 00:08:40,750
Now, we focus a lot
more on embodied AI,

175
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:44,920
which is adding the action
dimension to the problem.

176
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:47,800
We are studying problems like
visual navigation, manipulation,

177
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:50,740
rearrangement, embodied
question answering, instruction

178
00:08:50,740 --> 00:08:52,150
following.

179
00:08:52,150 --> 00:08:55,160
And the simulators replace--

180
00:08:55,160 --> 00:08:59,410
in some sense replace the
original role of data sets.

181
00:08:59,410 --> 00:09:02,650
One thing that doesn't
change, which is the data

182
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:04,190
is still super important.

183
00:09:04,190 --> 00:09:07,960
We are still relying
on large amount of data

184
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:11,110
to learn this
intelligent behavior,

185
00:09:11,110 --> 00:09:16,480
no matter it's from a static
data set or from a simulator.

186
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:21,880
So learning a simulation can
take a lot of interactions.

187
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:26,320
So just to give you an
example, we create this iGibson

188
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:29,830
environment, and we want to
learn a behavior called "Go

189
00:09:29,830 --> 00:09:31,640
into a room through
a closed door."

190
00:09:31,640 --> 00:09:34,220
So this is a rather
simple behavior,

191
00:09:34,220 --> 00:09:37,280
which I can show on the
top right of the screen.

192
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:40,570
So the agent needs to
stop in front of the door.

193
00:09:40,570 --> 00:09:42,260
It needs to stop at
the right distance.

194
00:09:42,260 --> 00:09:45,680
If it stops too close to the
door, it cannot extend its arm.

195
00:09:45,680 --> 00:09:47,590
If it's too far, it
cannot open the door.

196
00:09:47,590 --> 00:09:49,880
And then it basically
opens the door.

197
00:09:49,880 --> 00:09:50,935
Let me play this again.

198
00:09:50,935 --> 00:09:52,130
It opens the door.

199
00:09:52,130 --> 00:09:55,180
When there is enough clearance,
it will go into the door.

200
00:09:55,180 --> 00:10:00,010
However, it takes about 50,000
episodes or 1.25 million

201
00:10:00,010 --> 00:10:03,790
environment interactions to
learn this type of behavior.

202
00:10:03,790 --> 00:10:06,400
This is because we are using
model-free reinforcement

203
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:07,300
learning.

204
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:10,130
The agent is exploring
this environment.

205
00:10:10,130 --> 00:10:11,780
It could really push any point.

206
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:13,940
It could rather
stop at any point.

207
00:10:13,940 --> 00:10:18,330
So we give it a reward
function to go into the room.

208
00:10:18,330 --> 00:10:23,270
But it's very rare that it will
stumble upon this behavior.

209
00:10:23,270 --> 00:10:25,320
I would like to argue
with foundation models,

210
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:27,690
we can do a lot more different.

211
00:10:27,690 --> 00:10:29,750
So what do you do?

212
00:10:29,750 --> 00:10:31,490
Nowadays, you just ask ChatGPT.

213
00:10:31,490 --> 00:10:33,590
How do you go into a room
through a closed door?

214
00:10:33,590 --> 00:10:34,970
And it will say, open the door.

215
00:10:34,970 --> 00:10:35,970
Walk through the door.

216
00:10:35,970 --> 00:10:39,390
So this is a gross
simplification of the problem.

217
00:10:39,390 --> 00:10:43,320
Of course, the problem
is not that simple.

218
00:10:43,320 --> 00:10:45,680
But what I'm just
saying is that we

219
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:50,480
can leverage a lot of semantic
prior from the foundation

220
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:51,180
models.

221
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:54,680
So if we really lack data, if
we really need a lot of data,

222
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:57,410
the foundation model
is a compressed version

223
00:10:57,410 --> 00:10:59,840
of the entire data, and
it's a knowledge base

224
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:03,380
that you can query to accelerate
the development of robotics.

225
00:11:03,380 --> 00:11:05,840
Of course, simulation
and real world data

226
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:07,460
is still super, super important.

227
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:10,590
But maybe we can get
the best of both worlds.

228
00:11:10,590 --> 00:11:13,730
We can use foundation
models, plus a limited

229
00:11:13,730 --> 00:11:16,700
amount of simulation
or real world data.

230
00:11:16,700 --> 00:11:20,120
So that's what I'm going
to talk about today.

231
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:23,990
So where are we in terms of
foundation models plus robotics?

232
00:11:23,990 --> 00:11:26,600
So our team at
Google DeepMind has

233
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:29,730
been piloting in foundation
model plus robotics.

234
00:11:29,730 --> 00:11:33,410
So we developed advanced
planning, high-level planning

235
00:11:33,410 --> 00:11:34,520
algorithms.

236
00:11:34,520 --> 00:11:37,100
One of the first is
called PaLM-SayCan.

237
00:11:37,100 --> 00:11:41,160
It is an algorithm that
can parse a user command.

238
00:11:41,160 --> 00:11:42,218
So here is a demo.

239
00:11:42,218 --> 00:11:43,010
Here is a scenario.

240
00:11:43,010 --> 00:11:44,370
Here is a user command.

241
00:11:44,370 --> 00:11:45,750
I spilled my Coke on the table.

242
00:11:45,750 --> 00:11:47,030
How would you throw
it away and bring me

243
00:11:47,030 --> 00:11:48,170
something to help clean?

244
00:11:48,170 --> 00:11:50,030
And it's querying
a large language

245
00:11:50,030 --> 00:11:54,300
model, which is given a
score, highlighted in blue.

246
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:56,190
And there's also an
affordance score.

247
00:11:56,190 --> 00:11:59,180
The affordance will tell you
whether an action at a given

248
00:11:59,180 --> 00:12:00,420
state is possible.

249
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:01,970
It's augmenting
the language model

250
00:12:01,970 --> 00:12:04,680
to give you only
possible things.

251
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:07,400
So essentially, it is
doing the planning,

252
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:09,590
doing the semantic planning
with the language model,

253
00:12:09,590 --> 00:12:12,840
but it's also taking into
consideration what it can do.

254
00:12:12,840 --> 00:12:17,630
So it's not just outputting--

255
00:12:17,630 --> 00:12:20,880
the language model
tends to hallucination.

256
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:22,360
It doesn't hallucinate.

257
00:12:22,360 --> 00:12:24,660
It only gives you what is
possible for the robot to do

258
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:26,410
and what is actionable
for the robot.

259
00:12:26,410 --> 00:12:29,250
And the robot is doing the
thing that is advancing

260
00:12:29,250 --> 00:12:31,750
the long horizon task progress.

261
00:12:31,750 --> 00:12:36,270
And also, each task is
executed by a low-level policy.

262
00:12:36,270 --> 00:12:39,540
Here, it doesn't
quite clean the table

263
00:12:39,540 --> 00:12:42,775
because we haven't added
this to the low-level skill.

264
00:12:42,775 --> 00:12:44,400
But imagine, there
is a low-level skill

265
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:45,220
to clean the table.

266
00:12:45,220 --> 00:12:48,500
It will finish the entire thing.

267
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:50,840
What is the local
policy used to hear?

268
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:55,880
The local policy used to hear
is Robotic Transformer 1.

269
00:12:55,880 --> 00:12:59,250
It's our team's home
grown transformer.

270
00:12:59,250 --> 00:13:01,670
Essentially, we collect
a large data set

271
00:13:01,670 --> 00:13:04,140
of human demonstrations.

272
00:13:04,140 --> 00:13:09,550
We put the transformer, and
we train it on this large data

273
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:12,080
set of expert trajectories.

274
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:17,190
It is able to do about 700
tasks with 97% success rate.

275
00:13:17,190 --> 00:13:21,150
And it has interesting
generalization behavior.

276
00:13:21,150 --> 00:13:25,010
It can operate in a new kitchen
it has never seen before,

277
00:13:25,010 --> 00:13:29,390
which is showing there is a
successful recipe to apply

278
00:13:29,390 --> 00:13:31,440
foundation models in robotics.

279
00:13:31,440 --> 00:13:34,850
So that's roughly where are we
in terms of foundation model

280
00:13:34,850 --> 00:13:36,080
plus robotics.

281
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:41,780
And I will talk about a few
new works that is bringing this

282
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:44,270
to the next level.

283
00:13:44,270 --> 00:13:50,100
So actually, my teammate Ted
gave a talk of foundation models

284
00:13:50,100 --> 00:13:53,280
plus robotics at the
beginning of this year.

285
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:55,870
It's also this class CS25.

286
00:13:55,870 --> 00:13:56,920
I highly recommend it.

287
00:13:56,920 --> 00:13:58,620
It's available on YouTube.

288
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:01,200
I actually watched it
last night so that I don't

289
00:14:01,200 --> 00:14:03,930
repeat some of the contents.

290
00:14:03,930 --> 00:14:08,310
But what he basically
mentioned is that we--

291
00:14:08,310 --> 00:14:12,270
he revealed our team's
progress in terms of building

292
00:14:12,270 --> 00:14:14,550
this robotic foundation models.

293
00:14:14,550 --> 00:14:17,880
And we have had a lot
of somewhat detour,

294
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:20,880
and now we started to
figure out a recipe.

295
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:25,830
So in 2021 to 2022 is how
we scale to many tasks

296
00:14:25,830 --> 00:14:27,010
with demonstrations.

297
00:14:27,010 --> 00:14:28,960
How do we collect a
large amount of data?

298
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:33,460
In fact, about 100,000
demonstrations.

299
00:14:33,460 --> 00:14:36,490
And we tried different
ways to do it.

300
00:14:36,490 --> 00:14:37,897
We tried the behavior cloning.

301
00:14:37,897 --> 00:14:40,230
We tried imitation learning
plus reinforcement learning,

302
00:14:40,230 --> 00:14:43,380
and some other ways,
or combining them

303
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:46,230
with language models,
such as SayCan.

304
00:14:46,230 --> 00:14:50,490
In 2022 to 2023, it's about
how we can leverage foundation

305
00:14:50,490 --> 00:14:52,000
models to accelerate robotics.

306
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:56,160
We really see a proliferation
of using foundation models

307
00:14:56,160 --> 00:14:59,670
to accelerate robotics, both
on the high-level planning

308
00:14:59,670 --> 00:15:02,190
and low-level control,
probably leaning more

309
00:15:02,190 --> 00:15:04,170
towards the high-level planning.

310
00:15:04,170 --> 00:15:07,800
So if the recipe works--

311
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:11,370
so the recipe is essentially
combine a large scale,

312
00:15:11,370 --> 00:15:15,420
diverse offline data
set with a high capacity

313
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:17,250
architecture, such
as a transformer,

314
00:15:17,250 --> 00:15:19,870
and using language
as a universal glue.

315
00:15:19,870 --> 00:15:23,400
So this would be the recipe
to build foundation models

316
00:15:23,400 --> 00:15:25,370
for robotics.

317
00:15:25,370 --> 00:15:27,900
So if this recipe
works, what do we do?

318
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:28,800
What do we do next?

319
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:30,500
Essentially, we
just-- let's just

320
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:33,080
scale everything to
two orders of magnitude

321
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:37,230
and be done with it,
and solve robotics.

322
00:15:37,230 --> 00:15:38,558
And guess what.

323
00:15:38,558 --> 00:15:39,350
That's what we did.

324
00:15:39,350 --> 00:15:40,725
So that's the end
of the lecture.

325
00:15:40,725 --> 00:15:43,490
Maybe we can cut this
a little bit short.

326
00:15:43,490 --> 00:15:45,620
And that's a joke.

327
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:47,210
That's not happening.

328
00:15:47,210 --> 00:15:51,290
So we are still on
our way on our quest

329
00:15:51,290 --> 00:15:53,400
to solve low-level
embodied intelligence.

330
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:58,190
When I talk to people that
you can use foundation models

331
00:15:58,190 --> 00:16:02,810
to do robotics, their
reaction would be it's mostly

332
00:16:02,810 --> 00:16:04,710
doing high-level reasoning.

333
00:16:04,710 --> 00:16:08,510
It doesn't do the low-level
manipulation really well.

334
00:16:08,510 --> 00:16:10,310
And that's for a reason.

335
00:16:10,310 --> 00:16:13,700
One of the reasons is
there's Moravec's paradox.

336
00:16:13,700 --> 00:16:15,680
Moravec's paradox
is the observation

337
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:17,990
that in artificial
intelligence and robotics,

338
00:16:17,990 --> 00:16:21,680
contrary to traditional
assumptions or our intuitions,

339
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:23,850
reasoning requires very
little computation.

340
00:16:23,850 --> 00:16:26,850
But sensorimotor control
and perception skills

341
00:16:26,850 --> 00:16:29,470
require enormous
compute resources.

342
00:16:29,470 --> 00:16:33,330
That is because as
biological creatures,

343
00:16:33,330 --> 00:16:38,310
we acquire the sensorimotor
skills through evolution.

344
00:16:38,310 --> 00:16:39,640
This is very different.

345
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:44,430
So we might not be
able to reason or do

346
00:16:44,430 --> 00:16:46,350
large amount of large
scale computation,

347
00:16:46,350 --> 00:16:51,520
but this sensorimotor control
is integral to our survival.

348
00:16:51,520 --> 00:16:54,910
So it's essentially
already learned in our DNA.

349
00:16:54,910 --> 00:16:57,490
But in robotics, it's
a little bit different.

350
00:16:57,490 --> 00:17:01,973
So the chips are very good at
doing reasoning and computation,

351
00:17:01,973 --> 00:17:03,390
but they are not
super good-- they

352
00:17:03,390 --> 00:17:04,829
haven't experienced the world.

353
00:17:04,829 --> 00:17:07,560
They haven't acquired
the sensorimotor skills

354
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:12,210
that are necessary for them
to do tasks in the real world.

355
00:17:12,210 --> 00:17:13,599
Here is an example.

356
00:17:13,599 --> 00:17:19,680
When the computer beat Kasparov,
basically, the human champion

357
00:17:19,680 --> 00:17:23,980
in chess, there is not a robot
arm moving the chess piece.

358
00:17:23,980 --> 00:17:27,210
It can beat the human
champion in chess,

359
00:17:27,210 --> 00:17:30,040
but there's still someone that
needs to move the chess piece.

360
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:32,400
Similarly, in the
AlphaGo moment,

361
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:34,590
when Lee Sedol was
beaten by AlphaGo.

362
00:17:34,590 --> 00:17:37,810
There's still someone who is
moving the chess piece for them.

363
00:17:37,810 --> 00:17:39,250
It's not a robot doing that.

364
00:17:39,250 --> 00:17:41,410
So this is showing
the reasoning is--

365
00:17:41,410 --> 00:17:45,280
the hard things are easy,
and the easy things are hard.

366
00:17:45,280 --> 00:17:47,770
There's another thing
that prevents us

367
00:17:47,770 --> 00:17:51,460
from using foundation
models more prevalently,

368
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:54,040
more in larger scale
in robotics, which

369
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:56,980
is the training data bias.

370
00:17:56,980 --> 00:18:00,400
The training data of foundation
models or large language models

371
00:18:00,400 --> 00:18:02,300
are mostly language tasks.

372
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:05,050
So it's perhaps not
that surprising it

373
00:18:05,050 --> 00:18:08,110
knows how to clean up a
kitchen, because maybe there

374
00:18:08,110 --> 00:18:11,350
are wikiHow articles teaching
you how to clean up a kitchen,

375
00:18:11,350 --> 00:18:13,520
or to do something
in a procedural way.

376
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:16,240
But there is no wikiHow
articles teaching you

377
00:18:16,240 --> 00:18:18,850
how to move your finger 5
centimeters to the left,

378
00:18:18,850 --> 00:18:21,590
because people just
don't say that.

379
00:18:21,590 --> 00:18:22,940
People don't write that down.

380
00:18:22,940 --> 00:18:24,640
So there is a very
limited amount

381
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:29,010
of this low-level control data
in large language model training

382
00:18:29,010 --> 00:18:29,510
corpora.

383
00:18:29,510 --> 00:18:31,120
So we do have a
lot of challenges

384
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:33,800
in bringing the foundation
models to a lower level.

385
00:18:33,800 --> 00:18:37,240
So that's what I mean by
low-level embodied intelligence.

386
00:18:37,240 --> 00:18:39,800
So any questions so far?

387
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:41,920
I also want to make
this quite interactive.

388
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:44,110
So if there is any
questions, feel

389
00:18:44,110 --> 00:18:45,550
free to interrupt me any time.

390
00:18:45,550 --> 00:18:50,030

391
00:18:50,030 --> 00:18:52,820
All right, if not,
we can continue.

392
00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:54,560
So there are a
couple of challenges

393
00:18:54,560 --> 00:18:57,530
of using large language
models for low-level control.

394
00:18:57,530 --> 00:19:01,310
As I just mentioned, the
first thing is lack of data.

395
00:19:01,310 --> 00:19:08,820
So we only have perhaps 100,000
episodes of human demonstration

396
00:19:08,820 --> 00:19:09,320
data.

397
00:19:09,320 --> 00:19:13,050
And it takes about 13
robots 17 months to collect.

398
00:19:13,050 --> 00:19:15,680
So it's a huge amount of effort.

399
00:19:15,680 --> 00:19:19,250
On the contrary, large language
models are trained on the order

400
00:19:19,250 --> 00:19:20,970
of 1,000 billion tokens.

401
00:19:20,970 --> 00:19:25,970
PaLM, smaller PaLM, was trained
on 780 billion parameters--

402
00:19:25,970 --> 00:19:27,960
780 billion tokens.

403
00:19:27,960 --> 00:19:31,100
And the larger one is trained--

404
00:19:31,100 --> 00:19:33,020
following the
Chinchilla rule, you

405
00:19:33,020 --> 00:19:36,420
would need to train it
on 1.35 trillion tokens.

406
00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:40,910
So it's a huge
amount of discrepancy

407
00:19:40,910 --> 00:19:43,160
between how much data we
can achieve in robotics

408
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:47,690
and how much we can get
in large language models.

409
00:19:47,690 --> 00:19:50,280
So we will always be
bounded by robotic data.

410
00:19:50,280 --> 00:19:53,260
So maybe we can scale
on other fronts.

411
00:19:53,260 --> 00:19:55,580
Maybe we can keep the
robotics data the same,

412
00:19:55,580 --> 00:19:58,190
and then we can scale
on other fronts.

413
00:19:58,190 --> 00:20:00,080
Like maybe we can scale
the pre-training mix

414
00:20:00,080 --> 00:20:03,260
of text and image, or
maybe image and text pairs.

415
00:20:03,260 --> 00:20:07,740
Maybe we can build this
cake, and the robotics data

416
00:20:07,740 --> 00:20:09,790
is just a cherry on top of it.

417
00:20:09,790 --> 00:20:13,950
And we can scale the
foundation really, really well.

418
00:20:13,950 --> 00:20:16,770
Some of my work that I'm
going to talk about today

419
00:20:16,770 --> 00:20:18,790
actually reused the RT-1 data.

420
00:20:18,790 --> 00:20:20,640
We don't collect
new data for RT-2.

421
00:20:20,640 --> 00:20:25,890
But we want to do more things
with the same amount of data.

422
00:20:25,890 --> 00:20:30,170
The second challenge is kind of
related to the first challenge.

423
00:20:30,170 --> 00:20:34,290
Language models lack an
interface for low-level control.

424
00:20:34,290 --> 00:20:37,650
If you ask a language model,
"How do you make a robot dog

425
00:20:37,650 --> 00:20:40,800
stand up on two feet?", it will
tell you a lot of things that

426
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:42,940
sound reasonable,
sound plausible.

427
00:20:42,940 --> 00:20:45,510
It will tell you the robot
dog's torso is upright,

428
00:20:45,510 --> 00:20:49,540
balanced over two hind feet, and
standing shoulder width apart.

429
00:20:49,540 --> 00:20:50,260
This is great.

430
00:20:50,260 --> 00:20:52,468
This is all great, but we
cannot put it on the robot.

431
00:20:52,468 --> 00:20:55,720

432
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:57,940
On the other hand, maybe
we can ask a language model

433
00:20:57,940 --> 00:21:00,700
to write control code to
directly control the robot.

434
00:21:00,700 --> 00:21:03,760
But usually, that
requires you to curate

435
00:21:03,760 --> 00:21:07,010
an API that is friendly
to the language model.

436
00:21:07,010 --> 00:21:10,570
If you directly ask it to
give you my joint angles

437
00:21:10,570 --> 00:21:12,165
to make the robot
stand upright, it

438
00:21:12,165 --> 00:21:13,540
will not give you
the right thing

439
00:21:13,540 --> 00:21:15,440
because it doesn't
have enough context.

440
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:18,010
So essentially,
large language models

441
00:21:18,010 --> 00:21:20,440
don't speak robot language.

442
00:21:20,440 --> 00:21:24,110
Can we actually find the
right robot language?

443
00:21:24,110 --> 00:21:26,950
Can we find the interface
between large language models

444
00:21:26,950 --> 00:21:28,160
and robot control?

445
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:31,790
Or can we just treat robot
action as another language?

446
00:21:31,790 --> 00:21:34,780
So that's what we
want to find out.

447
00:21:34,780 --> 00:21:37,750
So in today's agenda,
I will be talking

448
00:21:37,750 --> 00:21:39,970
about low-level embodied
intelligence with foundation

449
00:21:39,970 --> 00:21:40,720
models.

450
00:21:40,720 --> 00:21:44,170
It's separated into
two parts, and it's

451
00:21:44,170 --> 00:21:47,200
addressing the two challenges
that I've just mentioned.

452
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:50,320
Part one is about model
consolidation, joint scaling,

453
00:21:50,320 --> 00:21:51,680
and positive transfer.

454
00:21:51,680 --> 00:21:54,340
So I have to put
them in one part

455
00:21:54,340 --> 00:21:56,180
because they are
somewhat related.

456
00:21:56,180 --> 00:21:59,260
And part two is developing a
new interface of large language

457
00:21:59,260 --> 00:22:00,550
models.

458
00:22:00,550 --> 00:22:03,650
So what do I mean by
model consolidation?

459
00:22:03,650 --> 00:22:06,280
Model consolidation--
yes, question--

460
00:22:06,280 --> 00:22:08,860
Yeah, I'm just going to ask
why couldn't you just fine tune

461
00:22:08,860 --> 00:22:14,800
an LLM for generating specific
low-level code, either some text

462
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:18,460
description [INAUDIBLE].

463
00:22:18,460 --> 00:22:19,160
Yeah.

464
00:22:19,160 --> 00:22:20,118
That's a good question.

465
00:22:20,118 --> 00:22:23,350
So the question is, why cannot
we fine tune a language model

466
00:22:23,350 --> 00:22:29,660
to directly output low-level
code or like robot actions?

467
00:22:29,660 --> 00:22:31,900
So I would be talking
about RT-2, which

468
00:22:31,900 --> 00:22:33,740
does somewhat similar to that.

469
00:22:33,740 --> 00:22:36,800
It fine tunes a language
model to output action

470
00:22:36,800 --> 00:22:40,910
as a language to output
our action representation.

471
00:22:40,910 --> 00:22:43,370
There are certain downsides to
that, like, for example, you

472
00:22:43,370 --> 00:22:47,150
would need to collect additional
data to fine tune a language

473
00:22:47,150 --> 00:22:48,530
model.

474
00:22:48,530 --> 00:22:51,920
So either we can fine tune that,
or we can use a language model

475
00:22:51,920 --> 00:22:54,532
zero shot if you find the
right interface, which

476
00:22:54,532 --> 00:22:56,490
I will talk about a little
bit in the part two.

477
00:22:56,490 --> 00:22:58,220
[INAUDIBLE] without fine tuning?

478
00:22:58,220 --> 00:23:00,860
Without fine tuning, yeah.

479
00:23:00,860 --> 00:23:03,807
So the model consolidation
is-- essentially,

480
00:23:03,807 --> 00:23:06,140
we can do the high-level
reasoning and low-level control

481
00:23:06,140 --> 00:23:07,020
in one model.

482
00:23:07,020 --> 00:23:11,210
And joint scaling is not only
we scale the robot data, which

483
00:23:11,210 --> 00:23:15,650
is expensive, we also scale
the pre-training data,

484
00:23:15,650 --> 00:23:18,980
or we already start from a
pre-trained vision language

485
00:23:18,980 --> 00:23:21,680
model, and the positive
transfer is the model benefiting

486
00:23:21,680 --> 00:23:25,910
from diverse joint training
across internet scale, language,

487
00:23:25,910 --> 00:23:31,300
vision, and vision language
domains combined with robotics.

488
00:23:31,300 --> 00:23:35,350
So this is a
continuation of the axes

489
00:23:35,350 --> 00:23:39,830
that Ted drew in
his previous talk.

490
00:23:39,830 --> 00:23:42,320
So we can see there is a trend.

491
00:23:42,320 --> 00:23:45,370
So this visualization
basically highlights

492
00:23:45,370 --> 00:23:47,230
some of the work of our team.

493
00:23:47,230 --> 00:23:52,480
And each work, each
column is basically

494
00:23:52,480 --> 00:23:55,630
a robotic system that is able
to do both high-level reasoning

495
00:23:55,630 --> 00:23:56,950
and low-level control.

496
00:23:56,950 --> 00:24:02,890
So previously, we needed to have
separate models for each thing.

497
00:24:02,890 --> 00:24:06,190
Previously, in the
initial release of SayCan,

498
00:24:06,190 --> 00:24:08,710
the planning is done by
a large language model,

499
00:24:08,710 --> 00:24:16,450
and the affordance is done
by TQ-Opt, like policy

500
00:24:16,450 --> 00:24:17,870
trained with sim2real.

501
00:24:17,870 --> 00:24:23,350
And the low-level policy
is robotic transformer one.

502
00:24:23,350 --> 00:24:27,550
So it's each model doing
its dedicated thing.

503
00:24:27,550 --> 00:24:32,090
And we need to train each
model differently and perhaps

504
00:24:32,090 --> 00:24:34,080
with different types of data.

505
00:24:34,080 --> 00:24:36,740
And later, we have
Q-transformer, which unifies,

506
00:24:36,740 --> 00:24:43,160
which is a kind of an offline
RL method that is leveraging

507
00:24:43,160 --> 00:24:44,490
transformer architecture.

508
00:24:44,490 --> 00:24:46,800
So it's a high
capacity architecture.

509
00:24:46,800 --> 00:24:49,230
It can train on both positive
data and negative data.

510
00:24:49,230 --> 00:24:53,600
And with that, we are able to
gather a policy that is also

511
00:24:53,600 --> 00:24:55,880
understanding affordances.

512
00:24:55,880 --> 00:24:58,680
So we can unify the low-level
policy and affordances.

513
00:24:58,680 --> 00:25:00,990
But the planning is still
a large language model.

514
00:25:00,990 --> 00:25:04,400
And then we have PaLM-E,
which is a vision language

515
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:07,460
model, which is a
large language model,

516
00:25:07,460 --> 00:25:09,810
also trained on vision
language domain.

517
00:25:09,810 --> 00:25:12,830
So the PaLM-E can do
planning and affordance

518
00:25:12,830 --> 00:25:16,110
in just one model, but the
low level is still using RT-1.

519
00:25:16,110 --> 00:25:18,540
And finally, we unify
everything together,

520
00:25:18,540 --> 00:25:20,960
like there is RT-2,
which I'm going

521
00:25:20,960 --> 00:25:23,690
to talk about today, that can
do both high-level planning

522
00:25:23,690 --> 00:25:25,700
to some extent,
generating affordance,

523
00:25:25,700 --> 00:25:28,080
and do low-level policies.

524
00:25:28,080 --> 00:25:30,650
So behind the
model consolidation

525
00:25:30,650 --> 00:25:33,480
is the consolidation of tasks.

526
00:25:33,480 --> 00:25:39,120
We can represent every task as
a vision plus text to text task.

527
00:25:39,120 --> 00:25:42,570
So it's a really universal
representation of the task.

528
00:25:42,570 --> 00:25:45,020
And then with
that, you can train

529
00:25:45,020 --> 00:25:47,720
it really using a lot of data.

530
00:25:47,720 --> 00:25:49,560
And you can see
positive transfer.

531
00:25:49,560 --> 00:25:53,000
Basically learning
affordance can also

532
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:56,610
tell you how to achieve a task.

533
00:25:56,610 --> 00:26:00,120
There are a transfer
between tasks when you

534
00:26:00,120 --> 00:26:03,480
pull all the tasks together.

535
00:26:03,480 --> 00:26:06,240
So to understand
this joint scaling

536
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:08,700
and to understand the
model consolidation,

537
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:12,100
we need to understand
PaLM-E a little bit.

538
00:26:12,100 --> 00:26:15,120
So PaLM-E is an embodied
multi-modal language model.

539
00:26:15,120 --> 00:26:17,380
It's based on the
PaLM architecture.

540
00:26:17,380 --> 00:26:19,300
So PaLM is a large
language model.

541
00:26:19,300 --> 00:26:22,860
We made some adaptation
on the architecture

542
00:26:22,860 --> 00:26:25,570
so it can understand
multimodal input.

543
00:26:25,570 --> 00:26:30,900
So it is basically
one model that

544
00:26:30,900 --> 00:26:34,480
is able to take in
multimodal input.

545
00:26:34,480 --> 00:26:38,460
So in large language
models, each word

546
00:26:38,460 --> 00:26:42,330
is tokenized, and
tokenized, and getting

547
00:26:42,330 --> 00:26:45,450
this embedding of these words.

548
00:26:45,450 --> 00:26:48,870
And then that is fed into
a large language model.

549
00:26:48,870 --> 00:26:53,040
So in PaLM-E, what we do
is instead of using words,

550
00:26:53,040 --> 00:26:55,930
we can use multimodal tokens.

551
00:26:55,930 --> 00:26:57,750
So the multimodal
tokens can come

552
00:26:57,750 --> 00:26:59,670
from a vision
language-- can come

553
00:26:59,670 --> 00:27:02,040
from a vision
transformer, a ViT,

554
00:27:02,040 --> 00:27:05,930
or it can come from
robot sensory data.

555
00:27:05,930 --> 00:27:11,320
So every multimodal
token, then we

556
00:27:11,320 --> 00:27:14,320
map it to the text
embedding space.

557
00:27:14,320 --> 00:27:18,340
We basically train
a linear affine

558
00:27:18,340 --> 00:27:23,680
transform between the multimodal
token and the text embedding

559
00:27:23,680 --> 00:27:24,290
space.

560
00:27:24,290 --> 00:27:29,230
And then we can treat the
multimodal token as words

561
00:27:29,230 --> 00:27:30,110
as well.

562
00:27:30,110 --> 00:27:35,800
So essentially, we have a
language model as a solid base,

563
00:27:35,800 --> 00:27:39,710
and then we start to adapt it
to understand multimodal tokens.

564
00:27:39,710 --> 00:27:42,730
So this is quite interesting,
because we don't--

565
00:27:42,730 --> 00:27:45,910
it doesn't require a ton
of adaptation or fine

566
00:27:45,910 --> 00:27:49,940
tuning for it to understand
multimodal input.

567
00:27:49,940 --> 00:27:53,620
It just aligns naturally
to the multimodal input,

568
00:27:53,620 --> 00:27:54,910
such as images.

569
00:27:54,910 --> 00:27:58,150
I will show a couple of
examples of what it can do.

570
00:27:58,150 --> 00:28:01,610
And we can train the same way as
training large language models.

571
00:28:01,610 --> 00:28:05,770
So essentially, we can reuse the
same infrastructure and training

572
00:28:05,770 --> 00:28:10,330
algorithm and everything
to train this PaLM-E.

573
00:28:10,330 --> 00:28:13,180
A couple other things
we find along the way

574
00:28:13,180 --> 00:28:17,140
is positive transfer, which
I will share in a little bit.

575
00:28:17,140 --> 00:28:21,180
So I guess here I
also want to mention

576
00:28:21,180 --> 00:28:25,190
PaLM-E is one of the largest
models we have explored so far.

577
00:28:25,190 --> 00:28:27,880
It has 562 billion
parameters, which

578
00:28:27,880 --> 00:28:33,280
is by concatenating the PaLM 540
billion parameters and the 22

579
00:28:33,280 --> 00:28:34,240
billion ViT.

580
00:28:34,240 --> 00:28:38,930
And we find a lot of emergent
capabilities of these models.

581
00:28:38,930 --> 00:28:44,540
That is we haven't expected
during training time,

582
00:28:44,540 --> 00:28:46,270
but really, we can
prompt these models,

583
00:28:46,270 --> 00:28:48,700
and ask it to do
interesting things.

584
00:28:48,700 --> 00:28:52,810
We have also explored using
neural scene representation,

585
00:28:52,810 --> 00:28:59,440
basically, an object-centric
representation fed into PaLM-E.

586
00:28:59,440 --> 00:29:04,750
So object-centric
representation assigns one token

587
00:29:04,750 --> 00:29:06,760
to each object.

588
00:29:06,760 --> 00:29:09,010
And we find that
this representation

589
00:29:09,010 --> 00:29:11,590
is super helpful
for robot planning

590
00:29:11,590 --> 00:29:16,000
tasks, because traditional ViT
representation is based on grid,

591
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:18,670
and it doesn't have a full
understanding of layered objects

592
00:29:18,670 --> 00:29:20,440
and their relationships.

593
00:29:20,440 --> 00:29:25,960
We have done an extensive study
on the scaling performance

594
00:29:25,960 --> 00:29:28,810
and the catastrophic
forgetting performance,

595
00:29:28,810 --> 00:29:32,560
and all other interesting
experiments in the paper.

596
00:29:32,560 --> 00:29:34,490
So please refer to
the paper for more.

597
00:29:34,490 --> 00:29:39,010
So here I'm just showing some
interesting qualitative examples

598
00:29:39,010 --> 00:29:44,050
of some emergent capability
of PaLM-E that we found out.

599
00:29:44,050 --> 00:29:47,950
So first, we found this model
has some reasoning capability.

600
00:29:47,950 --> 00:29:51,607
You can give it an image and
ask it questions that requires

601
00:29:51,607 --> 00:29:52,690
a little bit of reasoning.

602
00:29:52,690 --> 00:29:55,030
And you can prompt this
with, let's think step

603
00:29:55,030 --> 00:29:59,740
by step, which is a technique
used to elicit reasoning

604
00:29:59,740 --> 00:30:01,280
in large language models.

605
00:30:01,280 --> 00:30:04,670
But here in multimodal language
models, you can do the same.

606
00:30:04,670 --> 00:30:07,790
I guess people are
also experimenting it

607
00:30:07,790 --> 00:30:09,470
these days with GPT-4V.

608
00:30:09,470 --> 00:30:12,410
You can also prompt it to think
step by step or count, row

609
00:30:12,410 --> 00:30:13,190
by row.

610
00:30:13,190 --> 00:30:16,700
But here this is before GPT-4V.

611
00:30:16,700 --> 00:30:19,100
And we were able
to elicit reasoning

612
00:30:19,100 --> 00:30:21,020
using some of the
interesting prompts, such

613
00:30:21,020 --> 00:30:24,710
as we can ask it, in
this photo, are there

614
00:30:24,710 --> 00:30:26,528
more cats or more dogs?

615
00:30:26,528 --> 00:30:27,570
Let's think step by step.

616
00:30:27,570 --> 00:30:29,870
And they found--
the PaLM-E found out

617
00:30:29,870 --> 00:30:31,820
there are an equal
amount of dogs and cats.

618
00:30:31,820 --> 00:30:35,150
And on the right,
given an image,

619
00:30:35,150 --> 00:30:37,800
can I go down this street
on a bicycle, yes or no?

620
00:30:37,800 --> 00:30:39,090
Let's think step by step.

621
00:30:39,090 --> 00:30:41,240
And the reply is, do not enter.

622
00:30:41,240 --> 00:30:42,680
Second, except the bicycles.

623
00:30:42,680 --> 00:30:44,610
Do not entry except bicycles.

624
00:30:44,610 --> 00:30:45,110
Yes.

625
00:30:45,110 --> 00:30:47,450
So it's doing this
modus of reasoning.

626
00:30:47,450 --> 00:30:52,400
And it's mixing this
understanding of symbols,

627
00:30:52,400 --> 00:30:55,170
and also mixing the
understanding of text.

628
00:30:55,170 --> 00:30:58,160
So this is quite amazing to me.

629
00:30:58,160 --> 00:31:00,560
To be honest, when
I first saw this,

630
00:31:00,560 --> 00:31:02,630
I didn't expect a
multimodal language

631
00:31:02,630 --> 00:31:04,480
model would be able to do that.

632
00:31:04,480 --> 00:31:08,380
And we also tried one thing,
which is traditionally

633
00:31:08,380 --> 00:31:12,220
very difficult to language
models, which is to tell a joke.

634
00:31:12,220 --> 00:31:13,990
Language models can
understand joke,

635
00:31:13,990 --> 00:31:16,780
but sometimes it just doesn't--

636
00:31:16,780 --> 00:31:20,230
it's not able to
tell you a joke, when

637
00:31:20,230 --> 00:31:22,660
it comes to the punchline,
because it's just

638
00:31:22,660 --> 00:31:25,420
trying to make something
that is plausible

639
00:31:25,420 --> 00:31:26,840
and sounds like a joke.

640
00:31:26,840 --> 00:31:28,660
And when it comes
to a punchline,

641
00:31:28,660 --> 00:31:30,650
it doesn't really
know what to say.

642
00:31:30,650 --> 00:31:33,010
So here I give it an
image, and I asked it

643
00:31:33,010 --> 00:31:35,560
to come up with a description.

644
00:31:35,560 --> 00:31:37,670
And then it comes
up with a joke.

645
00:31:37,670 --> 00:31:40,240
So this guides the language
model to think step by step.

646
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:44,050
And the description is a
donkey is carrying a dog, cat,

647
00:31:44,050 --> 00:31:44,880
and rooster.

648
00:31:44,880 --> 00:31:46,630
And the joke is, what
do you call a donkey

649
00:31:46,630 --> 00:31:47,870
with a rooster on his back?

650
00:31:47,870 --> 00:31:49,210
A rooster booster.

651
00:31:49,210 --> 00:31:50,210
It's so creative.

652
00:31:50,210 --> 00:31:53,050
Like, when I saw this,
I'm pleasantly surprised.

653
00:31:53,050 --> 00:31:54,380
And I searched online.

654
00:31:54,380 --> 00:31:56,300
I couldn't find
another joke like that.

655
00:31:56,300 --> 00:31:59,000
So it's actually an
original joke by PaLM-E.

656
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:01,920
And finally, we see some maths
reasoning with this model.

657
00:32:01,920 --> 00:32:04,630

658
00:32:04,630 --> 00:32:08,470
Basically, I give it a messy
menu from a pizza store.

659
00:32:08,470 --> 00:32:11,830
And I ask it, how much is--

660
00:32:11,830 --> 00:32:13,720
I'm just buying a pizza
for me and my friend.

661
00:32:13,720 --> 00:32:14,637
How much should I pay?

662
00:32:14,637 --> 00:32:15,710
Let's think step by step.

663
00:32:15,710 --> 00:32:20,920
And it's figuring out there is
a pizza, and there's a $9.99.

664
00:32:20,920 --> 00:32:23,320
And it tells you the price.

665
00:32:23,320 --> 00:32:26,630
In some of the answers,
it even calculates text.

666
00:32:26,630 --> 00:32:30,140
But the text is hallucinated,
so that doesn't work.

667
00:32:30,140 --> 00:32:32,290
All right, let's talk
about positive transfer.

668
00:32:32,290 --> 00:32:37,610
So apart from the amazing
capabilities-- amazing things

669
00:32:37,610 --> 00:32:41,450
that PaLM-E can do, it also has
interesting positive transfer

670
00:32:41,450 --> 00:32:42,680
behavior.

671
00:32:42,680 --> 00:32:47,990
So when we train PaLM-E on a
single domain, when we train it

672
00:32:47,990 --> 00:32:50,930
on just a single robotics
task, the performance

673
00:32:50,930 --> 00:32:52,260
is not super great.

674
00:32:52,260 --> 00:32:54,270
But when we pull all
the data together

675
00:32:54,270 --> 00:32:59,810
and we also include internet
scale visual language tasks,

676
00:32:59,810 --> 00:33:03,200
such as captioning,
visual question answering,

677
00:33:03,200 --> 00:33:05,140
it is able to do much better.

678
00:33:05,140 --> 00:33:08,840
So this shows that it's
important to mix all the data

679
00:33:08,840 --> 00:33:12,170
together and train it jointly.

680
00:33:12,170 --> 00:33:17,240
The internet scale data can
act as a regularizer for you

681
00:33:17,240 --> 00:33:20,440
to not forget the
representations.

682
00:33:20,440 --> 00:33:23,470
And those representations
are, in turn, very useful

683
00:33:23,470 --> 00:33:26,060
for robotics.

684
00:33:26,060 --> 00:33:27,950
So that's the positive
transfer results.

685
00:33:27,950 --> 00:33:29,810
And we start to see
more and more positive

686
00:33:29,810 --> 00:33:31,470
transfer in other
of our studies.

687
00:33:31,470 --> 00:33:32,390
Yes.

688
00:33:32,390 --> 00:33:36,200
So how much did that do to do
proactive, like in simulation

689
00:33:36,200 --> 00:33:37,230
or in real world?

690
00:33:37,230 --> 00:33:41,990
I think the playing with
sorting stuff in [? policy, ?]

691
00:33:41,990 --> 00:33:43,140
is very impressive.

692
00:33:43,140 --> 00:33:44,840
Right.

693
00:33:44,840 --> 00:33:46,140
That's a very good point.

694
00:33:46,140 --> 00:33:52,340
So for the-- so these
are all planning

695
00:33:52,340 --> 00:33:53,650
data, high-level planning.

696
00:33:53,650 --> 00:33:57,680

697
00:33:57,680 --> 00:33:59,880
Maybe let's just talk
about two things.

698
00:33:59,880 --> 00:34:04,700
So first of all, the sorting
results, the low-level policy

699
00:34:04,700 --> 00:34:08,280
is still using a
traditional controller.

700
00:34:08,280 --> 00:34:10,440
So it's using a
policy called LAVA.

701
00:34:10,440 --> 00:34:15,800
And that policy is trained
on 68,000 episodes.

702
00:34:15,800 --> 00:34:19,460
The high-level planning is
probably easier than you think,

703
00:34:19,460 --> 00:34:22,699
because it only needs to tell--

704
00:34:22,699 --> 00:34:25,840
put the-- it's giving commands
to the low-level policy.

705
00:34:25,840 --> 00:34:28,080
So it basically
only needs to say,

706
00:34:28,080 --> 00:34:31,080
put the red block
into top left corner.

707
00:34:31,080 --> 00:34:33,040
Put another red block
into the top left corner.

708
00:34:33,040 --> 00:34:39,179
So it's rather a quite standard
autoregressive language modeling

709
00:34:39,179 --> 00:34:39,820
task.

710
00:34:39,820 --> 00:34:43,020
The only thing you need
to do is to determine

711
00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:45,130
what task is not finished yet.

712
00:34:45,130 --> 00:34:47,790
So, for example, if the block
is already in the corner,

713
00:34:47,790 --> 00:34:50,679
it shouldn't call another policy
to move it to the corner again.

714
00:34:50,679 --> 00:34:52,860
So it's rather like
understanding--

715
00:34:52,860 --> 00:34:55,600
parsing the states and
understanding the states.

716
00:34:55,600 --> 00:34:59,190
So this high-level policy
only requires about 50 to 100

717
00:34:59,190 --> 00:35:00,640
demonstrations to learn.

718
00:35:00,640 --> 00:35:02,620
So it's quite
parameter efficient.

719
00:35:02,620 --> 00:35:03,480
And in the future--

720
00:35:03,480 --> 00:35:05,730
that's a very good question,
actually-- in the future,

721
00:35:05,730 --> 00:35:09,060
a lot of these tasks can
be taught in context.

722
00:35:09,060 --> 00:35:12,210
So maybe you just demonstrate
it once to the large language

723
00:35:12,210 --> 00:35:15,730
model, then it knows
how to do that.

724
00:35:15,730 --> 00:35:23,870
How can a large language model
know which low-level policy

725
00:35:23,870 --> 00:35:24,370
it needs?

726
00:35:24,370 --> 00:35:27,410
Yeah, this is through human
demonstration as well.

727
00:35:27,410 --> 00:35:31,120
So a human on a low level can
demonstrate a low-level policy

728
00:35:31,120 --> 00:35:33,850
by teleoperating a robot
to do a certain task.

729
00:35:33,850 --> 00:35:39,130
But on a high
level, it could also

730
00:35:39,130 --> 00:35:41,290
just give the low-level policy.

731
00:35:41,290 --> 00:35:44,110
Imagine your control
interface is through text.

732
00:35:44,110 --> 00:35:45,340
And then you can--

733
00:35:45,340 --> 00:35:47,530
as a human, you can also
guide a low-level policy

734
00:35:47,530 --> 00:35:49,250
to accomplish a task.

735
00:35:49,250 --> 00:35:53,800
And then that thing can then be
used to train a large language

736
00:35:53,800 --> 00:35:54,460
model.

737
00:35:54,460 --> 00:35:57,050
So that's for the sorting block.

738
00:35:57,050 --> 00:35:59,350
The second is a little
bit more interesting

739
00:35:59,350 --> 00:36:04,060
because the planning steps are
actually generated by PaLM.

740
00:36:04,060 --> 00:36:09,220
So we essentially distilled
PaLM plus this affordance model

741
00:36:09,220 --> 00:36:12,950
into PaLM-E. So that's a
little bit more interesting.

742
00:36:12,950 --> 00:36:16,020
That's like using the AI
data to bootstrap itself.

743
00:36:16,020 --> 00:36:20,280
That one has about
3,000 episodes.

744
00:36:20,280 --> 00:36:25,040
Also, not quite a lot, but it's
able to learn complex planning

745
00:36:25,040 --> 00:36:28,520
behavior, replanning
behavior, error recovery,

746
00:36:28,520 --> 00:36:29,910
which I will show in this slide.

747
00:36:29,910 --> 00:36:34,340
So with the PaLM-E as
a high-level planner,

748
00:36:34,340 --> 00:36:40,510
we are able to take the rice
chips out of the drawer.

749
00:36:40,510 --> 00:36:46,570
And there is a twist, which is I
will be messing with the robot.

750
00:36:46,570 --> 00:36:49,630
And so as it put it
onto the counter,

751
00:36:49,630 --> 00:36:51,460
I put it back to the drawer.

752
00:36:51,460 --> 00:36:56,690
And as it picked it up again,
and then I put it back again.

753
00:36:56,690 --> 00:36:58,370
So it's able to
understand the state.

754
00:36:58,370 --> 00:37:01,190
It's able to understand
my task is not finished.

755
00:37:01,190 --> 00:37:02,980
I cannot proceed
with the next task.

756
00:37:02,980 --> 00:37:06,760
Now, after I don't
mess with it anymore,

757
00:37:06,760 --> 00:37:11,160
it's able to close the drawer
and pick up the bag of chips.

758
00:37:11,160 --> 00:37:16,830
So PaLM-E is able to combine
affordance and planning in one

759
00:37:16,830 --> 00:37:20,670
model and do complex
reasoning of the scene

760
00:37:20,670 --> 00:37:22,230
and the environment.

761
00:37:22,230 --> 00:37:26,910
And interestingly, we can use
the exact same model checkpoint

762
00:37:26,910 --> 00:37:29,158
to do block sorting as well.

763
00:37:29,158 --> 00:37:30,700
So this is the same
model checkpoint.

764
00:37:30,700 --> 00:37:35,010
It can not only reason about
how to bring a bag of chips

765
00:37:35,010 --> 00:37:35,640
to a user.

766
00:37:35,640 --> 00:37:37,700
It can also sort blocks.

767
00:37:37,700 --> 00:37:43,380
And it's also responding to
adversarial perturbation,

768
00:37:43,380 --> 00:37:47,810
like if the user is putting
the block in the middle again,

769
00:37:47,810 --> 00:37:50,680
it's able to recover from that.

770
00:37:50,680 --> 00:37:52,660
So these are all coming
from the same model.

771
00:37:52,660 --> 00:37:57,250
And it can also tell jokes.

772
00:37:57,250 --> 00:38:03,220
So yeah, that's just the power
of vision language models.

773
00:38:03,220 --> 00:38:06,010
Now, we want to
go a level deeper.

774
00:38:06,010 --> 00:38:07,870
These are all visual
language models

775
00:38:07,870 --> 00:38:10,310
that are used for planning
or high-level reasoning.

776
00:38:10,310 --> 00:38:12,640
Can we use them for
low-level control?

777
00:38:12,640 --> 00:38:14,710
It turns out we can.

778
00:38:14,710 --> 00:38:18,160
And that's the RT-2 work, which
is the vision language action

779
00:38:18,160 --> 00:38:21,230
model that transfers web
knowledge to robotic control.

780
00:38:21,230 --> 00:38:22,680
What can it do?

781
00:38:22,680 --> 00:38:27,970
When asked, pick up
the extinct animal

782
00:38:27,970 --> 00:38:31,340
and it has a whole range
of objects on the table,

783
00:38:31,340 --> 00:38:32,560
it will pick up the dinosaur.

784
00:38:32,560 --> 00:38:36,790
So it can link
the extinct animal

785
00:38:36,790 --> 00:38:40,730
to dinosaur and to the action
to pick the dinosaur up.

786
00:38:40,730 --> 00:38:45,340
So it's really doing like this
emergent reasoning, and also

787
00:38:45,340 --> 00:38:47,510
the manipulation in
just the one model.

788
00:38:47,510 --> 00:38:50,770
And by the way,
this robot hasn't

789
00:38:50,770 --> 00:38:54,220
seen any of these before,
at least in the robot

790
00:38:54,220 --> 00:38:54,950
training data.

791
00:38:54,950 --> 00:38:58,410
It might have seen this
in the internet catalog.

792
00:38:58,410 --> 00:39:02,780
But it has never seen it in
the robotics training data.

793
00:39:02,780 --> 00:39:07,610
So it's quite interesting
how we need to evaluate

794
00:39:07,610 --> 00:39:10,400
these robots nowadays.

795
00:39:10,400 --> 00:39:13,310
So when we evaluate
language models,

796
00:39:13,310 --> 00:39:16,310
to prevent data
contamination, every time

797
00:39:16,310 --> 00:39:18,740
you need to give it new
questions, because, otherwise,

798
00:39:18,740 --> 00:39:21,870
it might have already
memorized it in its training.

799
00:39:21,870 --> 00:39:25,700
When we evaluate these robots,
we actually go to a dollar store

800
00:39:25,700 --> 00:39:29,220
to buy all these toys to make
sure it hasn't seen that before.

801
00:39:29,220 --> 00:39:32,120
And as we run more
evaluations, maybe there

802
00:39:32,120 --> 00:39:33,780
will be some
replication as well.

803
00:39:33,780 --> 00:39:37,700
But as you can see, it
is able to understand

804
00:39:37,700 --> 00:39:41,640
to pick up this dinosaur toy.

805
00:39:41,640 --> 00:39:42,960
How did we do that?

806
00:39:42,960 --> 00:39:44,780
So we start from
a vision language

807
00:39:44,780 --> 00:39:48,640
model that is trained
on internet scale data.

808
00:39:48,640 --> 00:39:52,840
And then we also combine it with
robotics action data, which is

809
00:39:52,840 --> 00:39:54,970
the RT-1 data, and we get RT-2.

810
00:39:54,970 --> 00:39:59,960
And we can dive deeper, a
little bit deeper into RT-2.

811
00:39:59,960 --> 00:40:02,540
So first of all, what is
a vision language model?

812
00:40:02,540 --> 00:40:05,890
A vision language
model is a transformer

813
00:40:05,890 --> 00:40:10,990
that takes in image and
text, and then outputs text.

814
00:40:10,990 --> 00:40:17,650
So within Google, there's
a vision language model

815
00:40:17,650 --> 00:40:21,340
called PaLI, which is
an encoder-decoder type

816
00:40:21,340 --> 00:40:22,400
of architecture.

817
00:40:22,400 --> 00:40:25,810
It's basically having a ViT
to understand images, and then

818
00:40:25,810 --> 00:40:30,810
a transformer encoder and
the transformer decoder.

819
00:40:30,810 --> 00:40:34,360
They encompass both the
visual and semantics

820
00:40:34,360 --> 00:40:35,490
to understand the world.

821
00:40:35,490 --> 00:40:39,930
In the robotics, we have to deal
with a lot of both of these.

822
00:40:39,930 --> 00:40:42,240
And the question
is, can we leverage

823
00:40:42,240 --> 00:40:45,810
the knowledge in the
visual language models

824
00:40:45,810 --> 00:40:48,540
and apply them to robotics?

825
00:40:48,540 --> 00:40:51,060
On the other hand,
we have the RT-1.

826
00:40:51,060 --> 00:40:52,650
If you want to learn
more about RT-1,

827
00:40:52,650 --> 00:40:59,050
you can listen to the previous
episode of this CS25 by Ted.

828
00:40:59,050 --> 00:41:01,740
So he gave a detailed
introduction on the RT-1.

829
00:41:01,740 --> 00:41:06,420
But the RT-1, if you
stand far enough,

830
00:41:06,420 --> 00:41:12,090
it is also a vision language
to action or something model.

831
00:41:12,090 --> 00:41:14,260
It takes in human instruction.

832
00:41:14,260 --> 00:41:16,140
It takes in the
current camera image.

833
00:41:16,140 --> 00:41:19,530
The camera image passes through
a film efficient net, which

834
00:41:19,530 --> 00:41:21,990
is tokenized into
81 tokens, and then

835
00:41:21,990 --> 00:41:24,840
going to a token learner,
which compresses everything

836
00:41:24,840 --> 00:41:26,170
into eight tokens.

837
00:41:26,170 --> 00:41:28,620
And then there is
a transformer block

838
00:41:28,620 --> 00:41:30,570
leveraging a lot of
self-attention layer,

839
00:41:30,570 --> 00:41:32,100
and then generates actions.

840
00:41:32,100 --> 00:41:34,560
The action is also tokenized.

841
00:41:34,560 --> 00:41:40,380
The robot has seven
degrees of freedom.

842
00:41:40,380 --> 00:41:45,180
Basically, the end
effector has six degrees

843
00:41:45,180 --> 00:41:47,040
of freedom, its
position and rotation,

844
00:41:47,040 --> 00:41:49,040
and the gripper
can open and close.

845
00:41:49,040 --> 00:41:52,480
And there is another
dimension representing

846
00:41:52,480 --> 00:41:54,280
terminate the episode or not.

847
00:41:54,280 --> 00:41:57,160
Terminating means my
task is already done.

848
00:41:57,160 --> 00:42:02,870
And we discretize every
dimension into 256 bins.

849
00:42:02,870 --> 00:42:05,750
And then we do cross
entropy loss on those bins.

850
00:42:05,750 --> 00:42:09,770
So that's RT-1
architecture in a nutshell.

851
00:42:09,770 --> 00:42:12,010
It's quite similar to
a vision language model

852
00:42:12,010 --> 00:42:13,730
with different output tokens.

853
00:42:13,730 --> 00:42:16,000
So it's rather
natural that we just

854
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:19,390
use a large pre-trained
vision language model directly

855
00:42:19,390 --> 00:42:20,330
as policy.

856
00:42:20,330 --> 00:42:23,890
We can use the PaLI
or PaLM-E as a policy.

857
00:42:23,890 --> 00:42:26,980
And one question is, how
do we deal with actions

858
00:42:26,980 --> 00:42:29,680
when using pretrained
visual language models?

859
00:42:29,680 --> 00:42:33,250
And here is the actual
representation that we use.

860
00:42:33,250 --> 00:42:37,910
The robot actions here
are the eight dimensions.

861
00:42:37,910 --> 00:42:41,020
And as I mentioned, there's
termination, position change,

862
00:42:41,020 --> 00:42:42,190
and rotation change.

863
00:42:42,190 --> 00:42:46,850
And we discretize
everything into 256 bins.

864
00:42:46,850 --> 00:42:49,390
We also have tried other
alternative representations,

865
00:42:49,390 --> 00:42:53,180
but they are not as good as
just this naive representation.

866
00:42:53,180 --> 00:42:53,680
Yes.

867
00:42:53,680 --> 00:42:56,020
Can you go back to
the previous slide?

868
00:42:56,020 --> 00:42:57,000
Yeah.

869
00:42:57,000 --> 00:42:59,650
[INAUDIBLE],, what is
FILM EfficientNet?

870
00:42:59,650 --> 00:43:03,490
The FILM EfficientNet is a
pretrained convolutional neural

871
00:43:03,490 --> 00:43:04,310
network.

872
00:43:04,310 --> 00:43:06,860
It's used to
tokenize the images.

873
00:43:06,860 --> 00:43:10,637
So the reason that we do this
is through some ablation study,

874
00:43:10,637 --> 00:43:12,470
we can tokenize the
image in different ways.

875
00:43:12,470 --> 00:43:14,620
We can tokenize in ResNet.

876
00:43:14,620 --> 00:43:16,750
We can tokenize
everything into ResNet.

877
00:43:16,750 --> 00:43:19,810
And we can tokenize
using FILM EfficientNet.

878
00:43:19,810 --> 00:43:23,450
FILM, what it means is it
also takes into the language

879
00:43:23,450 --> 00:43:27,170
embedding and appends it
to the intermediate layers

880
00:43:27,170 --> 00:43:29,360
of the ResNet.

881
00:43:29,360 --> 00:43:31,400
So you basically have
some concatenation

882
00:43:31,400 --> 00:43:33,910
of whatever sentence
you put in in images?

883
00:43:33,910 --> 00:43:35,390
Yeah.

884
00:43:35,390 --> 00:43:36,973
That goes through
this neural network,

885
00:43:36,973 --> 00:43:38,098
this convolutional network?

886
00:43:38,098 --> 00:43:38,670
That's right.

887
00:43:38,670 --> 00:43:39,212
That's right.

888
00:43:39,212 --> 00:43:41,390
[INAUDIBLE] condition
[INAUDIBLE] transform it.

889
00:43:41,390 --> 00:43:44,758
Self-attention output
goes to action training.

890
00:43:44,758 --> 00:43:45,300
That's right.

891
00:43:45,300 --> 00:43:46,820
It's action as in the code.

892
00:43:46,820 --> 00:43:48,180
The action is not in code.

893
00:43:48,180 --> 00:43:51,680
The action is in text.

894
00:43:51,680 --> 00:43:53,683
It's basically
what is shown here.

895
00:43:53,683 --> 00:43:54,475
This is the action.

896
00:43:54,475 --> 00:43:57,140

897
00:43:57,140 --> 00:43:59,070
It's eight numbers.

898
00:43:59,070 --> 00:44:03,070
Each number ranges
from 0 to 255.

899
00:44:03,070 --> 00:44:07,250
And maybe another note
on the FILM ResNet.

900
00:44:07,250 --> 00:44:09,280
It's about how we
tokenize the images

901
00:44:09,280 --> 00:44:13,720
and how we combine vision
information and language

902
00:44:13,720 --> 00:44:14,640
information.

903
00:44:14,640 --> 00:44:16,310
There are many ways to do that.

904
00:44:16,310 --> 00:44:17,540
This is not the only way.

905
00:44:17,540 --> 00:44:19,810
There is early fusion
and late fusion,

906
00:44:19,810 --> 00:44:22,130
and there is also
cross-attention.

907
00:44:22,130 --> 00:44:25,010
You can basically tokenize
your image just by itself.

908
00:44:25,010 --> 00:44:28,090
And then you can have language
and use cross-attention

909
00:44:28,090 --> 00:44:30,950
to combine the image
and text representation.

910
00:44:30,950 --> 00:44:33,530
So here we are using this model.

911
00:44:33,530 --> 00:44:35,570
This is RT-1 for robotics.

912
00:44:35,570 --> 00:44:38,420
So we do have a lot of
considerations, such as latency.

913
00:44:38,420 --> 00:44:41,560
That's why we use this FILM
ResNet, because it's super fast.

914
00:44:41,560 --> 00:44:45,130
And it can output
a limited amount

915
00:44:45,130 --> 00:44:48,420
of tokens, which we can further
compress with a token learner.

916
00:44:48,420 --> 00:44:49,300
Yeah.

917
00:44:49,300 --> 00:44:52,270
And is this autoregressive,
like every single image

918
00:44:52,270 --> 00:44:54,580
it sees, it then [INAUDIBLE]?

919
00:44:54,580 --> 00:44:55,160
Right.

920
00:44:55,160 --> 00:44:57,190
So it is autoregressive, yeah.

921
00:44:57,190 --> 00:45:01,840
And every time we use a
history of up to six steps,

922
00:45:01,840 --> 00:45:04,370
so every time you see
this image right now,

923
00:45:04,370 --> 00:45:08,660
and you see about 2 seconds
of history before it.

924
00:45:08,660 --> 00:45:13,310
And this will be your input.

925
00:45:13,310 --> 00:45:15,290
Again, if you have more
questions about RT-1,

926
00:45:15,290 --> 00:45:19,880
I recommend watching
the previous episode.

927
00:45:19,880 --> 00:45:22,590
And here is all about RT-2.

928
00:45:22,590 --> 00:45:26,330
So we can convert the
string of numbers.

929
00:45:26,330 --> 00:45:30,020
This will be our output
of our transformer, which

930
00:45:30,020 --> 00:45:31,230
is a vision language model.

931
00:45:31,230 --> 00:45:35,040
We tried other alternatives,
such as floating numbers.

932
00:45:35,040 --> 00:45:38,330
Floating numbers is not super
friendly to language model

933
00:45:38,330 --> 00:45:42,420
tokenizer because it has
these decimal points.

934
00:45:42,420 --> 00:45:45,020
We also try the human language,
such as left or right.

935
00:45:45,020 --> 00:45:46,520
It's more a semantic
representation,

936
00:45:46,520 --> 00:45:49,400
but they cannot be directly
executed on a robot,

937
00:45:49,400 --> 00:45:53,150
which is a limitation
of this method.

938
00:45:53,150 --> 00:45:57,260
So if we commit to this action
representation, which is just

939
00:45:57,260 --> 00:45:59,240
a string of numbers,
we essentially

940
00:45:59,240 --> 00:46:01,040
get a vision language
action model.

941
00:46:01,040 --> 00:46:03,680
We tried different
variants, including

942
00:46:03,680 --> 00:46:09,260
PaLI-X. This is a Pathways
langauge image model.

943
00:46:09,260 --> 00:46:13,670
It can-- there are a
5-billion parameter variant

944
00:46:13,670 --> 00:46:15,660
and a 55-billion
parameter variant.

945
00:46:15,660 --> 00:46:19,700
And we also tried PaLM-E,
which is 12 billion parameters.

946
00:46:19,700 --> 00:46:24,320
The procedure that we
did to train this RT-2

947
00:46:24,320 --> 00:46:26,420
is via fine tuning.

948
00:46:26,420 --> 00:46:30,380
So fine tuning is to put
the internet scale data

949
00:46:30,380 --> 00:46:32,480
and the robotic data together.

950
00:46:32,480 --> 00:46:36,650
And then we fine tune it
on this mixture of data

951
00:46:36,650 --> 00:46:42,660
so that it retains the
internet scale knowledge.

952
00:46:42,660 --> 00:46:44,610
Maybe that's also an
artifact of our data

953
00:46:44,610 --> 00:46:46,600
is too small and
not diverse enough.

954
00:46:46,600 --> 00:46:48,630
So if you just fine
tune on robotics data,

955
00:46:48,630 --> 00:46:50,610
it will quickly
overfit and forget

956
00:46:50,610 --> 00:46:54,150
about all this
pretraining mixture.

957
00:46:54,150 --> 00:46:56,260
Maybe it's a dynamic of scale.

958
00:46:56,260 --> 00:46:59,490
So we'll see.

959
00:46:59,490 --> 00:47:02,190
At inference time,
how do we do this?

960
00:47:02,190 --> 00:47:05,640
We basically-- again, we
do this autoregressively.

961
00:47:05,640 --> 00:47:09,750
We have an
instruction of a task.

962
00:47:09,750 --> 00:47:12,960
And we format this as a
question and answering task.

963
00:47:12,960 --> 00:47:15,880
What should the robot do
to achieve a certain task?

964
00:47:15,880 --> 00:47:18,780
And the task is a string
that humans give the robot

965
00:47:18,780 --> 00:47:20,130
for the robot to achieve.

966
00:47:20,130 --> 00:47:26,390
And it also has the
current observation,

967
00:47:26,390 --> 00:47:30,920
which is the robot observation,
the camera image, RGB image,

968
00:47:30,920 --> 00:47:33,350
a pass through of
ViT, and then a pass

969
00:47:33,350 --> 00:47:36,830
through of the large language
model, and then output

970
00:47:36,830 --> 00:47:38,130
a list of tokens.

971
00:47:38,130 --> 00:47:42,650
So we leverage constraint
decoding to make sure it always

972
00:47:42,650 --> 00:47:48,200
has eight numbers,
because, otherwise,

973
00:47:48,200 --> 00:47:49,520
we cannot detokenize it.

974
00:47:49,520 --> 00:47:52,160
It's very easy for a language
model to just miss one number,

975
00:47:52,160 --> 00:47:52,660
right?

976
00:47:52,660 --> 00:47:56,930
So we do have some mechanism,
such as constraint decoding

977
00:47:56,930 --> 00:47:59,550
and beam search to make
sure the format is correct.

978
00:47:59,550 --> 00:48:01,490
After we get the string
of eight numbers,

979
00:48:01,490 --> 00:48:04,280
we detokenize it to
a delta t and delta

980
00:48:04,280 --> 00:48:07,100
r, which is the end
effector delta [INAUDIBLE]..

981
00:48:07,100 --> 00:48:10,370
And the robot can just
directly run this on the robot.

982
00:48:10,370 --> 00:48:13,070
After the run on the robot,
we repeat this process.

983
00:48:13,070 --> 00:48:15,770
We get another new image,
run through this process,

984
00:48:15,770 --> 00:48:16,850
and get the new action.

985
00:48:16,850 --> 00:48:20,900
And we repeat this process
until a termination is decoded.

986
00:48:20,900 --> 00:48:23,900
So some people
might be concerned

987
00:48:23,900 --> 00:48:26,220
that this is rather slow.

988
00:48:26,220 --> 00:48:29,600
It's, in fact, quite
slow, because it's

989
00:48:29,600 --> 00:48:32,970
12 billion parameters
or 5 billion parameters.

990
00:48:32,970 --> 00:48:34,890
We cannot run it on a robot.

991
00:48:34,890 --> 00:48:37,790
So we run on a TPU
cluster, and the robot

992
00:48:37,790 --> 00:48:40,310
is querying the TPU
cluster to get the numbers

993
00:48:40,310 --> 00:48:42,810
and apply it on the robot.

994
00:48:42,810 --> 00:48:45,170
So for the 12
billion parameters,

995
00:48:45,170 --> 00:48:47,880
we can actually run at 10 hertz.

996
00:48:47,880 --> 00:48:49,010
So it's quite fast.

997
00:48:49,010 --> 00:48:51,960
For all of the model, we
can run at least 3 hertz.

998
00:48:51,960 --> 00:48:57,370
So that is sufficient for
controlling our robot.

999
00:48:57,370 --> 00:49:02,770
And we see a lot of emerging
skills that is not trained--

1000
00:49:02,770 --> 00:49:04,360
that is not on the training set.

1001
00:49:04,360 --> 00:49:06,730
Essentially, as
I just mentioned,

1002
00:49:06,730 --> 00:49:09,202
we are probing what
this RT-2 can do.

1003
00:49:09,202 --> 00:49:10,160
We actually don't know.

1004
00:49:10,160 --> 00:49:12,327
So we are trying to figure
out what the RT-2 can do.

1005
00:49:12,327 --> 00:49:16,090
So we test it with a lot
of new tasks, such as,

1006
00:49:16,090 --> 00:49:18,280
put a strawberry into
the correct bowl,

1007
00:49:18,280 --> 00:49:21,310
or move banana to
Germany, like just

1008
00:49:21,310 --> 00:49:26,230
to test its understanding
of symbols or flags.

1009
00:49:26,230 --> 00:49:27,753
Pick a land animal.

1010
00:49:27,753 --> 00:49:28,420
There's a horse.

1011
00:49:28,420 --> 00:49:29,630
There's an octopus.

1012
00:49:29,630 --> 00:49:33,400
But basically, test the
semantic reasoning, and also

1013
00:49:33,400 --> 00:49:35,710
low-level manipulation skills.

1014
00:49:35,710 --> 00:49:41,920
And we divide the tasks
into symbol understanding,

1015
00:49:41,920 --> 00:49:44,990
and reasoning, and human
recognition, and average.

1016
00:49:44,990 --> 00:49:50,590
We found with RT-1, which is not
trained on internet scale data.

1017
00:49:50,590 --> 00:49:55,570
We do quite poorly in these
emergent evaluation tasks.

1018
00:49:55,570 --> 00:50:03,320
And in the RT-2
variants, which is fine

1019
00:50:03,320 --> 00:50:06,150
tuned on the internet
data and on robotics data,

1020
00:50:06,150 --> 00:50:08,130
we do much better
in these tasks.

1021
00:50:08,130 --> 00:50:11,010
And there is also
an effect of scale.

1022
00:50:11,010 --> 00:50:14,720
So the RT-2 was a
55 billion PaLI.

1023
00:50:14,720 --> 00:50:17,390
It's performing better
than the 12 billion PaLM-E.

1024
00:50:17,390 --> 00:50:20,370
Although, they perform quite
similarly for in-domain tasks.

1025
00:50:20,370 --> 00:50:23,280
But the generalization
is kind of interesting.

1026
00:50:23,280 --> 00:50:27,400
It seems with larger scale,
you can generalize better.

1027
00:50:27,400 --> 00:50:32,350
And here are some videos of the
robot achieving these tasks,

1028
00:50:32,350 --> 00:50:35,740
like moving the
banana to a number.

1029
00:50:35,740 --> 00:50:39,250
Put the strawberry
into the correct bowl.

1030
00:50:39,250 --> 00:50:41,780
Move a Rubik's cube
to the water bottle.

1031
00:50:41,780 --> 00:50:43,900
But I'm speaking Chinese.

1032
00:50:43,900 --> 00:50:46,750
Moving the banana
to a German flag.

1033
00:50:46,750 --> 00:50:51,830
So it's able to do all of
these very interesting tasks.

1034
00:50:51,830 --> 00:50:54,320
In terms of the
quantitative evaluations,

1035
00:50:54,320 --> 00:50:57,500
we also found that
the attitude policy

1036
00:50:57,500 --> 00:51:00,920
is quite robust to unseen
objects, unseen backgrounds,

1037
00:51:00,920 --> 00:51:03,440
and unseen environments.

1038
00:51:03,440 --> 00:51:07,010
And here is another evidence
of positive transfer.

1039
00:51:07,010 --> 00:51:10,430
So co-fine-tuned with
VQA data outperforms

1040
00:51:10,430 --> 00:51:12,270
fine-tuning on robotics only.

1041
00:51:12,270 --> 00:51:16,740
And if you train on robot data
from scratch, it barely works.

1042
00:51:16,740 --> 00:51:19,640
It almost doesn't work because
it overfits to robot data,

1043
00:51:19,640 --> 00:51:21,690
and our robot data
is just too small.

1044
00:51:21,690 --> 00:51:26,620
So we do need to do
co-fine-tuning, or at least

1045
00:51:26,620 --> 00:51:30,420
the fine-tuning so it retains
its internet scale knowledge.

1046
00:51:30,420 --> 00:51:33,410
This is also a
recipe for how people

1047
00:51:33,410 --> 00:51:36,600
would develop a domain
specific vision language model.

1048
00:51:36,600 --> 00:51:39,380
So you start from a very
general vision language model,

1049
00:51:39,380 --> 00:51:41,030
and you fine tune
on your domain,

1050
00:51:41,030 --> 00:51:45,450
or you can co-fine-tune with
your specific domain data.

1051
00:51:45,450 --> 00:51:48,080
This is likely a problem
that each vertical

1052
00:51:48,080 --> 00:51:54,450
of artificial intelligence
would incur someday.

1053
00:51:54,450 --> 00:51:56,730
We can also test
on other platforms,

1054
00:51:56,730 --> 00:51:58,780
like this shows some
cross embodiment.

1055
00:51:58,780 --> 00:52:02,580
The RT-2 PaLI-3B outperforms
previous models in terms

1056
00:52:02,580 --> 00:52:06,830
of moving blocks around
a 2D environment.

1057
00:52:06,830 --> 00:52:10,370
So this is-- and in
large language models,

1058
00:52:10,370 --> 00:52:12,530
we have this chain of
thought reasoning, which

1059
00:52:12,530 --> 00:52:17,833
is a method to elicit reasoning
in large language models.

1060
00:52:17,833 --> 00:52:19,250
You can either do
zero [INAUDIBLE]

1061
00:52:19,250 --> 00:52:20,330
chain-of-thought
reasoning by saying,

1062
00:52:20,330 --> 00:52:21,560
let's think step by step.

1063
00:52:21,560 --> 00:52:23,570
I'll give it the
examples of reasoning.

1064
00:52:23,570 --> 00:52:25,490
It's basically
decoding more things,

1065
00:52:25,490 --> 00:52:27,610
and then come to the conclusion.

1066
00:52:27,610 --> 00:52:31,970
We can use a similar procedure
for the RT-2 as well.

1067
00:52:31,970 --> 00:52:35,510
So in the RT-2 PaLM-E,
instead of directly decoding

1068
00:52:35,510 --> 00:52:37,910
the actions, we can
actually decode a plan,

1069
00:52:37,910 --> 00:52:40,110
and then append it with actions.

1070
00:52:40,110 --> 00:52:42,650
So this gives the language
model an opportunity

1071
00:52:42,650 --> 00:52:45,810
to understand the question or
parse the question differently.

1072
00:52:45,810 --> 00:52:48,530
It also gives us the
opportunity to reason

1073
00:52:48,530 --> 00:52:50,160
about things a little bit.

1074
00:52:50,160 --> 00:52:53,900
For example, if you say, bring
me a drink, and it will, say,

1075
00:52:53,900 --> 00:52:57,510
pick up a 7up can because
there's a 7up can on the table.

1076
00:52:57,510 --> 00:53:01,040
So we synthesized a couple of
hundred such examples using

1077
00:53:01,040 --> 00:53:03,740
a large language model, just
by augmenting the instruction,

1078
00:53:03,740 --> 00:53:07,090
and then fine tune the RT-2 just
for a couple of hundred steps.

1079
00:53:07,090 --> 00:53:11,070
So it's between for fine
tuning and in-context learning.

1080
00:53:11,070 --> 00:53:13,170
And it's able to
do some reasoning.

1081
00:53:13,170 --> 00:53:15,030
And some of the
interesting reasoning tasks

1082
00:53:15,030 --> 00:53:17,370
include, I need
to hammer a nail.

1083
00:53:17,370 --> 00:53:19,360
Which object from the
scene might be useful?

1084
00:53:19,360 --> 00:53:22,230
And in the scene,
there is a headphone.

1085
00:53:22,230 --> 00:53:22,920
There is a rock.

1086
00:53:22,920 --> 00:53:24,520
And there is a sticky note.

1087
00:53:24,520 --> 00:53:26,670
And the robot will
say, rocks, and then

1088
00:53:26,670 --> 00:53:28,390
generate actions to
pick up the rock.

1089
00:53:28,390 --> 00:53:31,260
So it's interesting
that it's able to do

1090
00:53:31,260 --> 00:53:34,000
this self-reasoning with RT-2.

1091
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:35,640
And here is a
demonstration of some

1092
00:53:35,640 --> 00:53:39,510
of the chain-of-thought
reasoning with RT-2 PaLM-E.

1093
00:53:39,510 --> 00:53:41,250
And the task is
pick up the thing

1094
00:53:41,250 --> 00:53:44,100
that is different from
all other objects.

1095
00:53:44,100 --> 00:53:47,422
And it picks up the chocolate
because this is a snack,

1096
00:53:47,422 --> 00:53:48,880
and the other things
are the drink.

1097
00:53:48,880 --> 00:53:51,340
And I can also speak
a different language.

1098
00:53:51,340 --> 00:53:54,750
And the plan would be to
translate it into a language

1099
00:53:54,750 --> 00:53:56,790
that it's familiar
with, which is English,

1100
00:53:56,790 --> 00:53:57,845
and then do the task.

1101
00:53:57,845 --> 00:54:01,020

1102
00:54:01,020 --> 00:54:03,153
There are also
potentially failure cases

1103
00:54:03,153 --> 00:54:04,570
of the chain-of-thought
reasoning.

1104
00:54:04,570 --> 00:54:06,580
So here, I say, move the
green objects together.

1105
00:54:06,580 --> 00:54:08,648
And as you can see,
the robot oscillates

1106
00:54:08,648 --> 00:54:10,440
between the two green
objects because there

1107
00:54:10,440 --> 00:54:11,380
are rather two plans.

1108
00:54:11,380 --> 00:54:14,400
It could move the can
to the bag of chips,

1109
00:54:14,400 --> 00:54:16,650
or it could move the
bag of chips to the can.

1110
00:54:16,650 --> 00:54:20,730
It oscillates between two
plans until one action,

1111
00:54:20,730 --> 00:54:22,260
bring it to an
object, and it will

1112
00:54:22,260 --> 00:54:26,140
commit to one of the
plans rather than another.

1113
00:54:26,140 --> 00:54:28,030
It's not always
guaranteed to work,

1114
00:54:28,030 --> 00:54:29,690
but it's quite interesting.

1115
00:54:29,690 --> 00:54:31,600
And it's also interesting
that, again, we

1116
00:54:31,600 --> 00:54:34,000
are testing the
manipulation policy,

1117
00:54:34,000 --> 00:54:38,800
like how we test intelligence
of humans, or animals, or kids,

1118
00:54:38,800 --> 00:54:43,560
because they're getting
more and more advanced.

1119
00:54:43,560 --> 00:54:45,360
As a summary, we have
the vision, language,

1120
00:54:45,360 --> 00:54:49,130
and action model that
is able to achieve--

1121
00:54:49,130 --> 00:54:50,600
improve the generalization.

1122
00:54:50,600 --> 00:54:53,410
It can do new tasks and
operate new objects.

1123
00:54:53,410 --> 00:54:55,620
It can also do
chain-of-thought reasoning.

1124
00:54:55,620 --> 00:54:58,890
And improving the
underlying model,

1125
00:54:58,890 --> 00:55:03,390
such as the vision language
model itself by scaling it up.

1126
00:55:03,390 --> 00:55:06,780
And trained with
internet scale data,

1127
00:55:06,780 --> 00:55:10,080
or trained with larger or higher
quality internet scale data,

1128
00:55:10,080 --> 00:55:11,940
we can achieve
better robot control,

1129
00:55:11,940 --> 00:55:14,970
which is quite amazing,
because robotics field has

1130
00:55:14,970 --> 00:55:16,890
been traditionally
developing quite slowly

1131
00:55:16,890 --> 00:55:18,360
and is bounded by
hardware, bounded

1132
00:55:18,360 --> 00:55:21,010
by a lot of different
things, bounded by operation.

1133
00:55:21,010 --> 00:55:24,750
But now it seems we can
piggyback on the development

1134
00:55:24,750 --> 00:55:26,850
of the foundation model field.

1135
00:55:26,850 --> 00:55:29,670
And whatever they do will
trickle down to our field

1136
00:55:29,670 --> 00:55:30,310
as well.

1137
00:55:30,310 --> 00:55:32,880
And the future will be to
increase the motion diversity

1138
00:55:32,880 --> 00:55:35,430
and extend on the
chain-of-thought reasoning

1139
00:55:35,430 --> 00:55:39,480
capability, and many more.

1140
00:55:39,480 --> 00:55:44,410
And so there is another example
of positive transfer, which

1141
00:55:44,410 --> 00:55:46,510
you might have seen recently.

1142
00:55:46,510 --> 00:55:49,730
So far I've been talking about
scaling differently, right?

1143
00:55:49,730 --> 00:55:52,630
I've been talking about
don't scale robotics data

1144
00:55:52,630 --> 00:55:53,980
and scale other data.

1145
00:55:53,980 --> 00:55:56,720
That's because robotics
data is so hard to collect.

1146
00:55:56,720 --> 00:56:00,230
And the purpose is not to
avoid collecting robotics data.

1147
00:56:00,230 --> 00:56:04,090
It's to develop a recipe
that you can do more

1148
00:56:04,090 --> 00:56:05,600
with limited robotics data.

1149
00:56:05,600 --> 00:56:08,050
However, there's also
an effort from our team

1150
00:56:08,050 --> 00:56:12,820
and the entire robotics
field to scale up

1151
00:56:12,820 --> 00:56:16,730
the robot data collection, which
is called the Open X Embodiment.

1152
00:56:16,730 --> 00:56:19,755
And the model train is called
RTX, Robotics Transformer

1153
00:56:19,755 --> 00:56:25,780
X. It's basically 22 type of
embodiments, and 572 skills,

1154
00:56:25,780 --> 00:56:28,365
and 60 data sets
pulled all together.

1155
00:56:28,365 --> 00:56:29,740
So this will be
the ultimate data

1156
00:56:29,740 --> 00:56:33,250
set we can use to study
positive transfer and to scale--

1157
00:56:33,250 --> 00:56:36,750
to study this joint scaling.

1158
00:56:36,750 --> 00:56:41,850
And there are already
evidences of positive transfer.

1159
00:56:41,850 --> 00:56:47,190
So we pulled all the data
together from all these labs

1160
00:56:47,190 --> 00:56:49,920
and find a common
action representation

1161
00:56:49,920 --> 00:56:52,840
that we can use to train
our robotic transformer.

1162
00:56:52,840 --> 00:56:57,180
And we have already found
this jointly trained model can

1163
00:56:57,180 --> 00:57:00,420
outperform a task-specific
model that is

1164
00:57:00,420 --> 00:57:02,440
developed in each of the labs.

1165
00:57:02,440 --> 00:57:05,040
So there is some benefits
in pulling all of the data

1166
00:57:05,040 --> 00:57:05,680
together.

1167
00:57:05,680 --> 00:57:12,260
So scaling robot data
is also quite important.

1168
00:57:12,260 --> 00:57:15,050
So the summary for this
part is that we are

1169
00:57:15,050 --> 00:57:16,620
having a model consolidation.

1170
00:57:16,620 --> 00:57:20,180
We can now do the high-level
reasoning and low-level control

1171
00:57:20,180 --> 00:57:21,180
in one model.

1172
00:57:21,180 --> 00:57:22,670
And the low-level
control part is

1173
00:57:22,670 --> 00:57:25,160
what excites me,
because it's so far

1174
00:57:25,160 --> 00:57:28,980
away from the traditional
language model domain.

1175
00:57:28,980 --> 00:57:30,470
It's so different.

1176
00:57:30,470 --> 00:57:34,040
And it shows signs of life
that we can trickle down

1177
00:57:34,040 --> 00:57:37,370
a lot more than we used
to think is possible.

1178
00:57:37,370 --> 00:57:40,370
And we can scale the pretraining
of visual language models,

1179
00:57:40,370 --> 00:57:42,240
as well as scaling
robotics data.

1180
00:57:42,240 --> 00:57:44,180
And we observe more
and more positive

1181
00:57:44,180 --> 00:57:47,630
transfer-- model benefiting
from diverse joint training

1182
00:57:47,630 --> 00:57:49,753
across internet-scale
language, vision,

1183
00:57:49,753 --> 00:57:50,920
and vision-language domains.

1184
00:57:50,920 --> 00:57:54,520

1185
00:57:54,520 --> 00:57:58,630
So I noticed that we are
close to running out of time.

1186
00:57:58,630 --> 00:58:01,950
So I will just very quickly go
through the second part, which

1187
00:58:01,950 --> 00:58:03,390
I think is also interesting.

1188
00:58:03,390 --> 00:58:06,490
It's to find new interfaces
of language models.

1189
00:58:06,490 --> 00:58:09,720
But I would only talk
at a very high level.

1190
00:58:09,720 --> 00:58:11,820
So language models,
as we can see,

1191
00:58:11,820 --> 00:58:13,560
can directly output
action tokens

1192
00:58:13,560 --> 00:58:15,610
if we found an action
representation.

1193
00:58:15,610 --> 00:58:19,700
So we can treat an action as yet
another language to the language

1194
00:58:19,700 --> 00:58:20,200
model.

1195
00:58:20,200 --> 00:58:21,580
So a language model
can do translation.

1196
00:58:21,580 --> 00:58:23,580
So it should be able to
generate action as well.

1197
00:58:23,580 --> 00:58:26,435
But that requires fine tuning.

1198
00:58:26,435 --> 00:58:27,810
Can we do it
without fine tuning,

1199
00:58:27,810 --> 00:58:31,090
or can we generate
more expressive actions

1200
00:58:31,090 --> 00:58:34,180
that is beyond the
scope of fine tuning?

1201
00:58:34,180 --> 00:58:37,840
So that is about finding
the right interface.

1202
00:58:37,840 --> 00:58:40,710
So previously, we have
already established

1203
00:58:40,710 --> 00:58:43,540
that language model doesn't
have an action interface.

1204
00:58:43,540 --> 00:58:48,060
If it has an action interface,
it's not as effective.

1205
00:58:48,060 --> 00:58:50,460
So what is the best
interface between language

1206
00:58:50,460 --> 00:58:51,820
and a lot of actions?

1207
00:58:51,820 --> 00:58:56,200
I would argue the best interface
between a language model

1208
00:58:56,200 --> 00:59:00,880
and a lot of actions
is reward functions.

1209
00:59:00,880 --> 00:59:04,070
And reward functions
is universal.

1210
00:59:04,070 --> 00:59:06,380
It has been used in
reinforcement learning.

1211
00:59:06,380 --> 00:59:10,870
And it's also a
reparameterization of actions.

1212
00:59:10,870 --> 00:59:11,720
What is action?

1213
00:59:11,720 --> 00:59:15,220
Let me-- let's see if I
want to pick up this bottle.

1214
00:59:15,220 --> 00:59:17,890
And I can say, what is a skill?

1215
00:59:17,890 --> 00:59:21,410
A skill is a mapping between
my observation and my action.

1216
00:59:21,410 --> 00:59:23,950
So the mapping between
my observation and action

1217
00:59:23,950 --> 00:59:25,120
can be seen as a skill.

1218
00:59:25,120 --> 00:59:27,700
But a skill can have an
alternative definition,

1219
00:59:27,700 --> 00:59:31,400
which is a set of constraints
and a set of objectives.

1220
00:59:31,400 --> 00:59:35,290
So picking up the bottle means
the bottle is in my right hand,

1221
00:59:35,290 --> 00:59:38,900
and the bottle is off
a supporting surface.

1222
00:59:38,900 --> 00:59:40,160
That means picking up.

1223
00:59:40,160 --> 00:59:42,670
And how do I pick it up
doesn't really matter.

1224
00:59:42,670 --> 00:59:47,110
That's a more-- to its broader
sense, the definition of skills

1225
00:59:47,110 --> 00:59:50,970
is more transferable
between different skills.

1226
00:59:50,970 --> 00:59:54,540
And the constraints
and objectives

1227
00:59:54,540 --> 00:59:57,280
can be represented as rewards.

1228
00:59:57,280 --> 01:00:01,450
So we can ask the language
model to generate these reward

1229
01:00:01,450 --> 01:00:02,210
functions.

1230
01:00:02,210 --> 01:00:04,367
And then there is an optimizer.

1231
01:00:04,367 --> 01:00:05,950
It could be reinforced
learning, or it

1232
01:00:05,950 --> 01:00:08,950
could be model
predictive control that

1233
01:00:08,950 --> 01:00:14,170
optimizes for those rewards,
and then run it on the robot.

1234
01:00:14,170 --> 01:00:18,160
So what is in the
reward translator?

1235
01:00:18,160 --> 01:00:19,310
Let's open a box.

1236
01:00:19,310 --> 01:00:21,520
So the reward
translator, basically,

1237
01:00:21,520 --> 01:00:23,290
is a two-stage process.

1238
01:00:23,290 --> 01:00:25,090
It's using the same
language model,

1239
01:00:25,090 --> 01:00:27,980
and it is using two
different prompts.

1240
01:00:27,980 --> 01:00:32,240
So the motion description
basically describes the motion.

1241
01:00:32,240 --> 01:00:36,070
So just now we found that
the language model can output

1242
01:00:36,070 --> 01:00:39,760
a description of how a
robot dog should stand up,

1243
01:00:39,760 --> 01:00:41,960
but it's not able
to achieve that.

1244
01:00:41,960 --> 01:00:44,415
But the motion description
is still sensible.

1245
01:00:44,415 --> 01:00:45,290
It still makes sense.

1246
01:00:45,290 --> 01:00:46,610
It gives you the right thing.

1247
01:00:46,610 --> 01:00:50,410
So we're just like, generate
this motion description,

1248
01:00:50,410 --> 01:00:53,590
and then we have a reward
translator, a reward coder that

1249
01:00:53,590 --> 01:00:55,540
translates this
motion description

1250
01:00:55,540 --> 01:01:02,230
into a piece of code that is
representing reward functions.

1251
01:01:02,230 --> 01:01:06,490
And these reward functions
cannot be directly executed

1252
01:01:06,490 --> 01:01:07,330
on the robot.

1253
01:01:07,330 --> 01:01:10,240
But it can go through
an optimization process

1254
01:01:10,240 --> 01:01:13,370
to learn how to achieve
those reward functions.

1255
01:01:13,370 --> 01:01:17,590
So we're using reward as the
interface between language model

1256
01:01:17,590 --> 01:01:19,510
and a low-level controller.

1257
01:01:19,510 --> 01:01:21,040
And for the
low-level controller,

1258
01:01:21,040 --> 01:01:26,170
we're using MuJoCo MPC, which
is a model predictive control

1259
01:01:26,170 --> 01:01:26,810
algorithm.

1260
01:01:26,810 --> 01:01:29,720
It's basically a
black box controller.

1261
01:01:29,720 --> 01:01:32,230
It samples a lot of
trajectories and finds

1262
01:01:32,230 --> 01:01:35,620
one that optimizes your reward.

1263
01:01:35,620 --> 01:01:39,760
And we tested it on a robot dog,
a quadruped robot, essentially,

1264
01:01:39,760 --> 01:01:41,510
and a dexterous manipulator.

1265
01:01:41,510 --> 01:01:44,870
So the dexterous manipulator
has an arm of six

1266
01:01:44,870 --> 01:01:48,880
or seven degrees of
freedom and a hand.

1267
01:01:48,880 --> 01:01:50,920
It's impossible to
control it because it

1268
01:01:50,920 --> 01:01:52,340
has so many degrees of freedom.

1269
01:01:52,340 --> 01:01:56,230
So it's highly challenging.

1270
01:01:56,230 --> 01:01:59,530
So just to showcase
some of the examples,

1271
01:01:59,530 --> 01:02:02,090
I omitted the motion
description part.

1272
01:02:02,090 --> 01:02:07,220
I only output the
reward code part.

1273
01:02:07,220 --> 01:02:09,550
So it seems that
the language model

1274
01:02:09,550 --> 01:02:11,860
is able to generate
the right reward

1275
01:02:11,860 --> 01:02:14,950
functions to make
the robot stand up

1276
01:02:14,950 --> 01:02:18,080
on two back feet like a human.

1277
01:02:18,080 --> 01:02:20,200
And now we are a little
bit more ambitious.

1278
01:02:20,200 --> 01:02:21,820
We know it can stand up.

1279
01:02:21,820 --> 01:02:23,740
Can we make the
robot do a moonwalk

1280
01:02:23,740 --> 01:02:25,190
while standing up like this?

1281
01:02:25,190 --> 01:02:27,010
So a moonwalk is
from Michael Jackson,

1282
01:02:27,010 --> 01:02:28,323
and it's very challenging.

1283
01:02:28,323 --> 01:02:29,740
How do we make the
robot to do it?

1284
01:02:29,740 --> 01:02:32,590
So it generates the
motion description

1285
01:02:32,590 --> 01:02:35,000
and generates the reward code.

1286
01:02:35,000 --> 01:02:39,170
But the motion is
not so correct-- not

1287
01:02:39,170 --> 01:02:40,880
exactly what we want.

1288
01:02:40,880 --> 01:02:42,680
The nice thing about
using a language model

1289
01:02:42,680 --> 01:02:45,930
and using the reward function
is that you can coach the robot.

1290
01:02:45,930 --> 01:02:48,890
You can go back and
explain what went wrong,

1291
01:02:48,890 --> 01:02:50,990
and ask the language
model to fix it.

1292
01:02:50,990 --> 01:02:53,120
So now we can actually say--

1293
01:02:53,120 --> 01:02:54,660
you're being very patient.

1294
01:02:54,660 --> 01:02:56,900
You say, moonwalk
means a robot should

1295
01:02:56,900 --> 01:02:59,330
walk backwards
while the feet swing

1296
01:02:59,330 --> 01:03:02,690
as if they are moving forward.

1297
01:03:02,690 --> 01:03:04,440
Such a great explanation.

1298
01:03:04,440 --> 01:03:05,480
Kudos to my colleague.

1299
01:03:05,480 --> 01:03:07,820
And correct your
answer, and also

1300
01:03:07,820 --> 01:03:11,160
make it work at the speed
of 0.5 meters per second.

1301
01:03:11,160 --> 01:03:13,310
And after you-- like
being very patient

1302
01:03:13,310 --> 01:03:14,750
and give it the
right instruction,

1303
01:03:14,750 --> 01:03:18,890
it's able to modify the
reward-- the motion descriptor

1304
01:03:18,890 --> 01:03:22,950
and also generate the right set
of rewards to make this happen.

1305
01:03:22,950 --> 01:03:25,850
And now you can teach
a robot to do moonwalk,

1306
01:03:25,850 --> 01:03:29,570
just by using the
language as an interface.

1307
01:03:29,570 --> 01:03:32,370
And one day we'll be able to do
this on the real robot as well.

1308
01:03:32,370 --> 01:03:33,200
Yes.

1309
01:03:33,200 --> 01:03:38,932
So the previous session, he
showed how the [INAUDIBLE]

1310
01:03:38,932 --> 01:03:40,640
big numbers, and you're
constraining them

1311
01:03:40,640 --> 01:03:42,020
to [? just ?] big numbers.

1312
01:03:42,020 --> 01:03:45,480
Here, how do you prevent it from
just hallucinating [INAUDIBLE]??

1313
01:03:45,480 --> 01:03:45,980
Right.

1314
01:03:45,980 --> 01:03:49,130
So that's a great question.

1315
01:03:49,130 --> 01:03:52,320
In this work, we are
not preventing it,

1316
01:03:52,320 --> 01:03:54,560
hallucination-- to
do hallucination

1317
01:03:54,560 --> 01:03:56,430
in a programmatic way.

1318
01:03:56,430 --> 01:04:00,200
We have a set of system
prompts or a set of rules

1319
01:04:00,200 --> 01:04:03,200
that is explaining the API.

1320
01:04:03,200 --> 01:04:05,330
After all, the
reward functions need

1321
01:04:05,330 --> 01:04:11,600
to be able to be compiled by
the optimizer, and then after--

1322
01:04:11,600 --> 01:04:13,730
so we do need to
have some check.

1323
01:04:13,730 --> 01:04:15,830
What's more, if it
doesn't compile,

1324
01:04:15,830 --> 01:04:18,530
we can just give the error
message to the language model.

1325
01:04:18,530 --> 01:04:20,780
It doesn't have to propagate
all the way to the motion

1326
01:04:20,780 --> 01:04:21,570
descriptor.

1327
01:04:21,570 --> 01:04:23,220
It can stay at the reward coder.

1328
01:04:23,220 --> 01:04:25,080
If there are errors,
please fix it.

1329
01:04:25,080 --> 01:04:28,730
So after that, it should
be able to fix it.

1330
01:04:28,730 --> 01:04:31,860
We can also chain
multiple tasks together.

1331
01:04:31,860 --> 01:04:35,580
Using this framework, we
can say, open the drawer.

1332
01:04:35,580 --> 01:04:36,750
Take the apple.

1333
01:04:36,750 --> 01:04:37,980
Put it into the drawer.

1334
01:04:37,980 --> 01:04:39,480
And close the door.

1335
01:04:39,480 --> 01:04:41,760
And it will be able to do that.

1336
01:04:41,760 --> 01:04:43,440
So we tried that.

1337
01:04:43,440 --> 01:04:46,080
Just using reward coder
is not good enough.

1338
01:04:46,080 --> 01:04:51,000
It's rather our two-stage prompt
is really, really helpful.

1339
01:04:51,000 --> 01:04:53,640
I think that's another
inspiration for other fields,

1340
01:04:53,640 --> 01:04:57,550
like when your domain is too
different from language domain,

1341
01:04:57,550 --> 01:05:00,510
maybe it would be good to find
an intermediate representation

1342
01:05:00,510 --> 01:05:02,460
and ask the language
model to explain

1343
01:05:02,460 --> 01:05:04,050
in that intermediate
representation

1344
01:05:04,050 --> 01:05:08,880
before it directly goes to a
more obscure representation.

1345
01:05:08,880 --> 01:05:12,180
And finally, we want to
transfer this to the real world.

1346
01:05:12,180 --> 01:05:13,800
But there is a challenge.

1347
01:05:13,800 --> 01:05:16,950
In simulation, it might
generate actions that

1348
01:05:16,950 --> 01:05:19,550
are too dexterous, like this.

1349
01:05:19,550 --> 01:05:23,520
This thing is not possible
to do in the real world.

1350
01:05:23,520 --> 01:05:28,720
So we add a few more regularizer
terms to stabilize the motion.

1351
01:05:28,720 --> 01:05:32,640
And we also run some state
estimation on the real robot

1352
01:05:32,640 --> 01:05:36,820
so that they understand
where is the cubes.

1353
01:05:36,820 --> 01:05:39,450
And then we can, in the
simulation, grab the motion,

1354
01:05:39,450 --> 01:05:41,680
and then achieve it
in the real world.

1355
01:05:41,680 --> 01:05:44,950
So here are some of the
execution in the real world.

1356
01:05:44,950 --> 01:05:47,400
So you can say, pick
up the Rubik's cube,

1357
01:05:47,400 --> 01:05:50,670
and it will generate
the motion to pick up

1358
01:05:50,670 --> 01:05:52,050
the Rubik's cube and the apple.

1359
01:05:52,050 --> 01:05:53,710
This is quite
different from RT-2.

1360
01:05:53,710 --> 01:05:56,820
The motions are quite smooth.

1361
01:05:56,820 --> 01:05:58,420
It's quite fast.

1362
01:05:58,420 --> 01:06:01,810
It's much faster than 3 hertz.

1363
01:06:01,810 --> 01:06:07,870
So here it can do 10
hertz, or even 30 hertz.

1364
01:06:07,870 --> 01:06:12,760
So it's comparable
with human speed.

1365
01:06:12,760 --> 01:06:15,230
All right, so that's
language to reward.

1366
01:06:15,230 --> 01:06:16,720
There's one last
thing that I want

1367
01:06:16,720 --> 01:06:20,390
to talk about in terms of
finding a new interface.

1368
01:06:20,390 --> 01:06:23,650
So a lot of time we have been
thinking about language model

1369
01:06:23,650 --> 01:06:26,360
as a semantic engine,
a semantic machine.

1370
01:06:26,360 --> 01:06:27,740
It understands semantics.

1371
01:06:27,740 --> 01:06:33,320
So, for example, you say the
student takes out the book.

1372
01:06:33,320 --> 01:06:34,090
You will say book.

1373
01:06:34,090 --> 01:06:37,550
A language model is able to
reason about such a sequence.

1374
01:06:37,550 --> 01:06:39,400
But if you do
low-level patterns,

1375
01:06:39,400 --> 01:06:42,560
like, if you just give
it obscure numbers,

1376
01:06:42,560 --> 01:06:43,540
what can you do?

1377
01:06:43,540 --> 01:06:45,430
It's actually a
low-level interface,

1378
01:06:45,430 --> 01:06:47,620
and we can open up the
low-level interface

1379
01:06:47,620 --> 01:06:51,830
to other language models and
ask it to do robotics tasks.

1380
01:06:51,830 --> 01:06:54,820
So in this paper, "Large
Language Models as General

1381
01:06:54,820 --> 01:06:58,060
Pattern Machines," we explore
using the low-level interface

1382
01:06:58,060 --> 01:07:02,260
of a large language model,
essentially asking it to reason

1383
01:07:02,260 --> 01:07:03,670
about different sequences.

1384
01:07:03,670 --> 01:07:05,870
And it's surprisingly
quite effective.

1385
01:07:05,870 --> 01:07:11,530
And it can solve tasks like
the ARC challenge and the PCFG.

1386
01:07:11,530 --> 01:07:13,862
And it can even do
sequence improvement.

1387
01:07:13,862 --> 01:07:16,070
So I will dig a little bit
into sequence improvement,

1388
01:07:16,070 --> 01:07:19,140
because that's quite
relevant to robotics.

1389
01:07:19,140 --> 01:07:22,310
So sequence improvement is that
you prompt the language model

1390
01:07:22,310 --> 01:07:25,130
with state, action,
and reward tuples.

1391
01:07:25,130 --> 01:07:28,160
And you just prompt
it with higher reward

1392
01:07:28,160 --> 01:07:31,070
and see if it can
generate actions

1393
01:07:31,070 --> 01:07:32,820
that achieve the higher reward.

1394
01:07:32,820 --> 01:07:34,730
So it's doing
reinforced learning,

1395
01:07:34,730 --> 01:07:38,370
or a reinforce learning
like thing, but in context.

1396
01:07:38,370 --> 01:07:39,570
So this is quite amazing.

1397
01:07:39,570 --> 01:07:44,120
So previously, you would need a
dedicated algorithm collecting

1398
01:07:44,120 --> 01:07:46,672
data, replay buffer to do
this reinforcement learning.

1399
01:07:46,672 --> 01:07:49,130
But now you can just build
everything in the language model

1400
01:07:49,130 --> 01:07:51,500
context by leveraging
the low-level interface

1401
01:07:51,500 --> 01:07:53,510
of a language model.

1402
01:07:53,510 --> 01:07:55,970
And with that, we can
actually do something

1403
01:07:55,970 --> 01:07:57,210
like clicker training.

1404
01:07:57,210 --> 01:07:59,600
So if we are not very familiar
with clicker training,

1405
01:07:59,600 --> 01:08:02,210
this is how you can do that.

1406
01:08:02,210 --> 01:08:04,807
You can have a
dog, and you can--

1407
01:08:04,807 --> 01:08:06,890
when it does the right
thing, you give it a reward

1408
01:08:06,890 --> 01:08:08,335
by clicking.

1409
01:08:08,335 --> 01:08:08,960
The other hand.

1410
01:08:08,960 --> 01:08:11,990
So you can-- so the
clicker training

1411
01:08:11,990 --> 01:08:16,069
is giving the agent a reward.

1412
01:08:16,069 --> 01:08:18,910
And we can now use
clicker training

1413
01:08:18,910 --> 01:08:20,029
to train robots as well.

1414
01:08:20,029 --> 01:08:22,611
So here the robot is exploring.

1415
01:08:22,611 --> 01:08:24,819
But I would give a click
when it does the right thing

1416
01:08:24,819 --> 01:08:26,120
or towards the right direction.

1417
01:08:26,120 --> 01:08:29,890
And over time, it
will be able to push

1418
01:08:29,890 --> 01:08:32,840
the bag of chips, which is the
objective of this training.

1419
01:08:32,840 --> 01:08:36,910
So you can do this entire
decision transformer-like

1420
01:08:36,910 --> 01:08:38,979
operation, but
purely in context,

1421
01:08:38,979 --> 01:08:41,890
by just giving a language
model a bunch of patterns

1422
01:08:41,890 --> 01:08:46,970
and ask it to figure out what is
the regularity of this sequence.

1423
01:08:46,970 --> 01:08:49,270
And this way, it can
generate new actions

1424
01:08:49,270 --> 01:08:51,195
to improve a previous sequence.

1425
01:08:51,195 --> 01:08:54,600

1426
01:08:54,600 --> 01:08:58,020
So for the language
model, we can

1427
01:08:58,020 --> 01:09:02,279
find new interfaces that are
more suitable for teaching

1428
01:09:02,279 --> 01:09:03,899
it low-level skills.

1429
01:09:03,899 --> 01:09:06,606
Reward is a bridge
of the language model

1430
01:09:06,606 --> 01:09:07,689
and the low-level control.

1431
01:09:07,689 --> 01:09:11,189
And we can fully leverage
it as a universal interface.

1432
01:09:11,189 --> 01:09:15,390
And we can optimize
in real time.

1433
01:09:15,390 --> 01:09:18,399
Sometimes it outperforms,
generating action directly.

1434
01:09:18,399 --> 01:09:22,140
So it really motivates to
use the reward functions

1435
01:09:22,140 --> 01:09:23,260
as an interface.

1436
01:09:23,260 --> 01:09:25,649
And in the language model
as general pattern machines,

1437
01:09:25,649 --> 01:09:28,359
we can use language model
beyond the semantic tasks.

1438
01:09:28,359 --> 01:09:30,149
We can ask it to reason
low level things.

1439
01:09:30,149 --> 01:09:34,770
And also, robotics as a domain
rich of sequence transformation,

1440
01:09:34,770 --> 01:09:37,420
and sequence completion, and
sequence improvement tasks.

1441
01:09:37,420 --> 01:09:40,109
So we can really
study the lower level

1442
01:09:40,109 --> 01:09:43,060
mechanisms of language models.

1443
01:09:43,060 --> 01:09:45,970
And the key takeaway is that--

1444
01:09:45,970 --> 01:09:50,470
for this talk is that we
are seeing more and more use

1445
01:09:50,470 --> 01:09:53,590
of foundation models, not
only on the semantic reasoning

1446
01:09:53,590 --> 01:09:57,880
side of robotics, but more on
the dexterous, on the generating

1447
01:09:57,880 --> 01:10:00,790
actions, on the
lower level embodied

1448
01:10:00,790 --> 01:10:02,930
intelligence side of robotics.

1449
01:10:02,930 --> 01:10:05,890
And we need to rethink
the scaling law

1450
01:10:05,890 --> 01:10:07,310
of robotics and transformer.

1451
01:10:07,310 --> 01:10:09,980
How do we scale it with
limited amount of data?

1452
01:10:09,980 --> 01:10:12,700
We have a new recipe for
scaling robot model and data

1453
01:10:12,700 --> 01:10:15,970
in RT-2, which shows that you
can do more with the same data.

1454
01:10:15,970 --> 01:10:18,830
With essentially RT-1
data plus internet data,

1455
01:10:18,830 --> 01:10:20,560
you can generalize
to a lot more things.

1456
01:10:20,560 --> 01:10:24,490
And RT-X shows that you can
do a lot more with more data.

1457
01:10:24,490 --> 01:10:26,830
There is also benefits to
collecting more robotics data.

1458
01:10:26,830 --> 01:10:28,940
And there is positive
transfers everywhere.

1459
01:10:28,940 --> 01:10:32,590
And part two, in terms of new
interfaces for language models,

1460
01:10:32,590 --> 01:10:35,860
I think it's worth
for the robotics field

1461
01:10:35,860 --> 01:10:38,080
to think about developing
new and lower level

1462
01:10:38,080 --> 01:10:41,020
interface to language
models, which facilitate

1463
01:10:41,020 --> 01:10:43,010
learning low level skills.

1464
01:10:43,010 --> 01:10:45,570
With that, I would like
to conclude my talk.

1465
01:10:45,570 --> 01:10:47,510
And if you find it
interesting, there

1466
01:10:47,510 --> 01:10:50,540
are a lot of references
for you to look into.

1467
01:10:50,540 --> 01:10:55,650
And special thanks to my team,
Google DeepMind Robotics Team.

1468
01:10:55,650 --> 01:10:58,040
So we are at the forefront
of developing foundation

1469
01:10:58,040 --> 01:10:59,540
models for robotics.

1470
01:10:59,540 --> 01:11:01,520
And stay tuned for
more in the future.

1471
01:11:01,520 --> 01:11:02,100
Thank you.

1472
01:11:02,100 --> 01:11:08,980

1473
01:11:08,980 --> 01:11:09,820
Yes.

1474
01:11:09,820 --> 01:11:11,800
You mentioned that
[INAUDIBLE] numbers

1475
01:11:11,800 --> 01:11:14,650
are difficult for
large language models.

1476
01:11:14,650 --> 01:11:18,530
But if you're just generating
the action tokens themselves,

1477
01:11:18,530 --> 01:11:21,010
like no [INAUDIBLE],,
or whatever you had,

1478
01:11:21,010 --> 01:11:24,520
an example, why don't you
just have a linear layer

1479
01:11:24,520 --> 01:11:28,990
appended to the transformer
that would just generate numbers

1480
01:11:28,990 --> 01:11:32,180
from 0 to 25 or whatever it is?

1481
01:11:32,180 --> 01:11:32,680
Yeah.

1482
01:11:32,680 --> 01:11:36,850
The question is that if we are--

